{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md'","aggregated_output":"# The published head trace: follow a publisher's index HEAD by principal\n\nStatus: **design proposed 2026-10-05; nothing here is implemented.** Author\nseat: claude-fable-5-1 (Lead System Architect). It awaits an independent\nreview by a different model family and the owner's answers to section 12.\nSubordinate to [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md),\n[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md),\n[`dao.stream.remote.md`](./dao.stream.remote.md),\n[`dao.jing.dht.md`](./dao.jing.dht.md) and\n[`yin.vm.linker.dht.md`](./yin.vm.linker.dht.md). It amends the last two\nwhere section 4 says so. In sections 5 to 7 every sentence is a rule.\n\n## 1. Objective\n\nA publisher deposits its index HEAD as a signed trace on a stream it alone\nwrites. A reader that declares the publisher's principal follows that trace,\nloads each index it names, and resolves `(require 'alib)` against the newest\nhead it has honoured. The reader is handed no manifest address: the join\ntoken shrinks to `yin:<host:port>/<principal>`.\n\nThis is the \"latest-root discovery\" that `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` sections 1\nand 13 and `dao.jing.dht.md` section 1 (owner decision 3) left to a later\nepic. It answers one question: given a principal and a peer address, what\nis that principal's current index, with every trusted claim stated?\n\nIt does not answer how a reader finds peers it was not given (rendezvous),\nand it changes nothing in how a name is resolved once an index is loaded.\n\n## 2. The owner's words\n\nVerbatim, 2026-10-05:\n\n> is it possible to start the yin.repl to always reference whatever the HEAD\n> is? (require 'alib) should always use the index manifest of the HEAD\n\n> publishing a's head is a what stigmergy is for. a can publish its head and\n> c can read it\n\nThe orchestrator's reading (a paraphrase, not the owner's words): the head\nis a trace A deposits in a shared medium and C perceives; it is not a\nmutable-pointer service C calls.\n\n**The reading holds.** No invariant contradicts it, and three require it\n(section 3: no server or client, single-writer provenance, a stream has no\nprivileged reader). One precision: across a network C's perception is\ncarried as the stream's own operations asked over a channel\n(`dao.stream.remote.md` section 2). Those are `cursor` and `next` on an\nordinary stream. No operation named after a head exists, and A's node\nanswers them without knowing what the stream holds.\n\n## 3. The invariants it rests on\n\n- **A host boundary is a stream boundary; a callback is an event on a\n  stream; a stream has no privileged reader and its meaning is not in it**\n  (`datom.world.md`, Streams and Host Boundaries). The head is a value on a\n  ring. The follower is one interpreter of it; a relay is another.\n- **Derive, don't persist** (`datom.world.md`, Design Principles). The\n  trace's sequence is the greatest transaction `t` of the index it names.\n  The publisher stores no counter and no trace.\n- **Sources stay separate db-values; union is explicit**\n  (`datom.world.md`, Tuples and Datoms). A followed index is never merged\n  into the reader's own index.\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics** (`datom.world.md`,\n  Architectural Commitments). The DHT keeps routing strict content\n  addresses and learns nothing about roots (`dao.jing.dht.md` section 1).\n- **Any stream may be exposed over WebSocket or UDP, peer to peer, through\n  a NAT; there is no server, no client and no privileged node**\n  (`dao.stream.remote.md` section 1, owner, 2026-09-25). The head ring is\n  one table entry; any node may serve one.\n- **A fetch has no deadline; liveness belongs to the drive through\n  `dao.lease`; the linker is clock-free** (owner, 2026-09-26;\n  `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 8.3). A silent publisher ends nothing. Every delay\n  below is counted in the node's appended ticks, as its repair delays\n  already are.\n- **`dao.stream.apply` is independent of rpc** (owner, 2026-09-29). The\n  head trace is not an apply operation and adds no `:dao.stream.apply/*`\n  word. It uses a ring, the mirror and a reflection.\n- **Plain Clojure first; `yin.repl` uses the same path through host\n  functions** (owner, 2026-10-01; `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` section 10, the\n  interface gate). Section 6 is that path.\n- **No backward compatibility** (owner). The three-part token and\n  hydration into HEAD are removed, not kept beside their successors.\n- **Single-writer provenance; the agent never addresses a recipient;\n  deposited traces are perceived by whoever observes** (`dao.agent.md`\n  invariant 2 and section 4.3). Invariants 1 and 3 bind nothing new here:\n  no host access is added to portable code beyond existing seams, and no\n  continuation is captured.\n- **Authority is the reader's composition; an index authenticates nothing**\n  (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 7.1). A head trace is honoured only for a\n  principal the reader declared.\n\n## 4. The starting tree: defects, gaps and deferrals\n\nRead at `ee3498de`. Three kinds, kept apart.\n\n**Architectural defects in the current design.**\n\n- **D1. Hydration makes the reader's HEAD a fork of the publisher's\n  index.** `dht join` installs the token's manifest as the reader's own\n  HEAD (`src/cljc/yin/repl/dht.cljc:202-212`) and rehydrates the reader's\n  indexer from it. The reader's next evaluated round then appends its own\n  rows to the publisher's history and moves the reader's HEAD past it.\n  A later publisher head can no longer be installed: `refused-head`\n  (`dht.cljc:46-53`) rightly refuses to replace an index the directory\n  holds. So \"follow the HEAD\" cannot be built on hydration at all.\n  Correction: a followed head is a snapshot beside the reader's HEAD\n  (section 5.5), never the reader's HEAD.\n- **D2. `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 7.2 says \"Loading an index is not a\n  subscription.\"** True of `load-index`, and it stays true. But 7.2 leaves\n  no place for a snapshot that follows a principal. Correction: 7.2 gains\n  a third member of the snapshot set, the honoured head of each followed\n  principal (section 5.5). Sections 1 and 13 of that document drop\n  \"latest-root discovery\" from their deferrals and point here.\n\n**Contradictions in the brief, stated instead of designed around.**\n\n- **C1. The signature cannot live in the `m` slot.** `datom.md` (line\n  168) fixes `m` as \"always an integer\", a reference to a local metadata\n  entity. A proof map in `m` is not a datom. The trace is a plain signed\n  value (section 5.2); a stream carries \"whatever values the consumer\n  needs: no datom requirement\" (`datom.world.md`, Streams).\n- **C2. A head under a key in the DHT contradicts `dao.jing.dht.md`\n  section 1**: \"There are no roots, no CAS records ... The DHT answers one\n  question ... It does nothing else.\" Section 8 rejects it.\n- **C3. `dao.stream.remote.md` 2.2 calls the descriptor's identity \"the\n  served logical stream\".** A reader holding only a principal cannot know\n  a ring's identity before it asks. Section 5.1 keys the table entry by a\n  conventional name instead. The implemented mirror already permits this\n  (`src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:148-163` looks the request's identity\n  up in the table and never compares it with the handle's own). The spec\n  needs one sentence saying so; slice H1 carries it.\n\n**Implementation gaps.**\n\n- G1. A loaded index does not survive a restart: the node's loads are\n  process state (`src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc`, `:loads`).\n- G2. A name whose resolved address moved under a live session is neither\n  relinked nor reported; a repeat `(require ...)` is silent.\n- G3. `announce!` (`dao/space/dht.cljc:387-393`) is unsigned, private and\n  discarded by `close!`. That is correct for what it is, the node's work\n  queue (section 5.4), and is no defect.\n- G4. The status lines of `dao.stream.remote.md` and `dao.stream.ws.md`\n  say \"design target, not implemented\" while `dao.stream.remote`,\n  `dao.stream.udp` and `dao.stream.ws-project` are in `src/cljc`. Doc\n  drift; H1 corrects the first.\n\n**Intentionally deferred, and still deferred:** section 11.\n\n## 5. The design\n\n### 5.1 Medium (decision 1)\n\n**The head trace lives on a ring the publisher alone writes, which its\nnode serves as one mirror-table entry on the datagram socket it already\nhas.**\n\n- The **head board** of a principal is a `dao.stream.ringbuffer` of\n  capacity 1 (section 5.3), created by the node's composition. Its\n  surface in the table is `#{:reader}`.\n- The table key is the conventional name\n  `\"yin.head/ed25519:<64 lowercase hex>\"`: the string `\"yin.head/\"`\n  followed by the principal id of `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 6.2. The ring\n  keeps its own per-process identity, which rides its cursors.\n- The channel is `dao.stream.udp` on the node's one socket.\n  `dao.stream.datagram.md` section 6 already lets `dao.jing.dht` and\n  `dao.stream.udp` share it: the DHT owns the maps carrying\n  `:dao.jing.dht/v`, the UDP channel every other decodable value. One\n  address, one NAT mapping, both interpreters, neither aware of the other.\n- So the token `yin:<host:port>/<principal>` is exactly a remote\n  descriptor:\n\n  ```clojure\n  {:dao.stream/type     :dao.stream/remote\n   :dao.stream/identity \"yin.head/ed25519:<hex>\"\n   :dao.stream/channel  {:dao.stream/type     :dao.stream/udp\n                         :dao.stream/identity <channel identity>\n                         :dao.stream.udp/host h\n                         :dao.stream.udp/port p}}\n  ```\n\n- Any other channel `dao.stream.remote.md` section 3 defines (WebSocket,\n  a relay pair) carries the same entry unchanged. The plain API takes\n  descriptors; only the token form presumes UDP.\n- **Nothing is put in the DHT.** The DHT is used as today, to fetch the\n  blobs of the index the trace names.\n\n**A relay may serve the trace, and needs no trust.** A trace is\nself-authenticating (section 5.2). Any node that follows a principal, has\na socket and is publishing (`:publish?`) enters its own capacity-1 ring\nunder the same name, holding the last trace it **installed**, verbatim. It\nis that node's stream, written by that node; the authorship of the value\ninside is the signature's. A fetch-only node serves nothing, as its banner\nalready promises. No node is a relay by kind: a relay is a follower with a\nsocket.\n\n**Availability.**\n\n- Publisher online and reachable: a reader observes a new head within one\n  poll interval (section 7).\n- Publisher offline, a relay reachable: the reader observes the last head\n  that relay installed. It can load that head's index only as far as the\n  DHT still holds the blobs (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 5.5.6).\n- Publisher offline, no relay: the reader observes nothing new. It keeps\n  the head it persisted and everything it linked. Nothing times out.\n- A reader that has never had contact and reaches no source has no head.\n  Section 5.6 says what a `require` does then.\n\nRejected: a signed mutable record under a DHT key (section 8, X1); both\nmedia at once (X2).\n\n### 5.2 Trace schema (decision 2)\n\nA trace is one plain value: an envelope and its proof, the shape a name\nassertion already has (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 6.1, 6.3).\n\n```clojure\n{:yin.head/envelope {:yin.head/principal \"ed25519:<64 lowercase hex>\"\n                     :yin.head/manifest  :segment/...\n                     :yin.head/seq       1042}\n :yin.head/proof    {:yin.head/signature \"<128 lowercase hex>\"}}\n```\n\n- The key sets are closed. No other key is written, and a trace carrying\n  one is malformed.\n- No time is written: the publisher owns no clock. No address is written:\n  a reply goes to the observed source (`dao.jing.dht.md` section 6), and a\n  relay serves from its own.\n- The signed message is exactly\n  `UTF-8(\"yin.head/trace:v1\\n\") || dao.jing/canonical-bytes(envelope)`.\n  The prefix is domain separation: a name-envelope signature is never a\n  head proof, and the reverse.\n- Signing and verification are `yin.vm.linker.sign`'s Ed25519 primitive,\n  unchanged, with the prefix as the only new datum. The principal is its\n  own key, so verification needs nothing but the trace.\n- Verification refuses, as `:yin.head/bad-proof`: a signature that does\n  not verify, a malformed key or signature, and a principal that is not\n  `\"ed25519:\"` plus a well-formed key.\n\n**The sequence is derived, on both sides.**\n\n- `:yin.head/seq` is the greatest transaction `t` among the datoms of the\n  index the manifest names. The publisher reads it from the round it just\n  committed. It stores no counter and no trace.\n- It survives a restart because the index does: `yin.repl.index/rehydrate`\n  preserves each datom's `t`, and the transactor derives the next `t` from\n  the restored log.\n- A reader verifies it. After the index is loaded, a trace whose sequence\n  is not the greatest `t` of the loaded datoms is refused\n  `:yin.head/seq-mismatch` and is never installed. The sequence orders\n  traces before the load; the index confirms it after.\n- A publisher never signs a second manifest at a sequence it has signed\n  in this process: the deposit is refused locally,\n  `:yin.head/seq-collision`. Slice H0 pins by test that two HEAD writes\n  of one directory never share a greatest `t`.\n- **The key kept and the directory lost** restarts `t`, and with it the\n  sequence, exactly as `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 6.5 says of the name\n  sequence. Readers refuse the new traces as stale. The remedy is that\n  section's: hydrate from the last published manifest before publishing,\n  or take a new key.\n\nRejected: the `m` slot (C1); a stored counter beside the key; a `:prev`\nhash chain (section 8, X3).\n\n### 5.3 Retention (decision 3)\n\n**Capacity 1. The latest head wins and the ring itself coalesces.**\n\n- No reader needs a superseded head. Loading one is waste, and a head\n  history is the index's own history, already content-addressed.\n- **First contact.** The reader mints `:dao.stream/oldest`. On a\n  capacity-1 ring the earliest retained position is the current head, so\n  the first `next` answers it. A ring nothing was deposited on answers\n  `blocked`.\n- **A slow reader** holding a cursor across two deposits is answered\n  `gap` with the recovery cursor, which is the current head's position.\n  It adopts it. The gap is the honest statement \"heads were skipped\", and\n  it costs nothing: skipping them is the policy.\n- **A restarted source** has a new ring. The reader's old cursor is\n  answered `cursor-mismatch`, a present answer; the reader mints\n  `:oldest` again. The same holds when a peer's entry disappears\n  (`not-found`) and returns.\n- A reader keeps one cursor per source and per principal. Cursors never\n  cross sources.\n\nRejected: a larger ring (nothing reads the extra elements); a\ncomplete-history log on a network-reachable entry (the wrong declared\nnature for a latest-wins value, `dao.stream.md`, Retention and Gaps).\n\n### 5.4 Publisher behaviour (decision 4)\n\n- **When.** One trace is deposited at every HEAD move: after the HEAD\n  write, in the same call, before any network result. This is the rule\n  HEAD itself follows (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 5.5.6), and it is what the\n  owner asked for: the head, whatever it is. The deposit is a local\n  append; no round waits.\n- **Coalescing is the medium's.** A publisher does not batch. Ten rounds\n  between two polls leave one trace.\n- **At startup** a node with a key and a recovered HEAD deposits the trace\n  of that HEAD at once: manifest from HEAD, sequence from the recovered\n  datoms, signature from the key. A reader's first contact after the\n  publisher restarts therefore observes the head without any new round.\n- **Who deposits.** A node deposits when it has a key, a socket and\n  `:publish?`. Without a key it signs nothing, as it publishes no name.\n  Without `:publish?` it serves no board: a head whose blobs it will not\n  serve is a promise it cannot keep.\n- **Relation to `announce!` and the ledger ring: beside, not reuse, wrap\n  or replace.** The ledger ring is the node's private work queue: every\n  put address and each window close, read once by `step`, for replication\n  accounting. It is unsigned because `dao.space.dht` holds no key and\n  knows nothing of `yin.*`, and both are right. Serving it would publish\n  every put address and still authenticate nothing. The head board is a\n  second ring with one job. The composition that calls `announce!` calls\n  the deposit beside it, in that order: `yin.repl.dht`'s `:head-fn`, and\n  the plain path `publish! -> assert! -> announce! -> deposit!`.\n- The publisher's own directory holds nothing new. HEAD stays the one\n  record of what its head is.\n\nRejected: deposit only on `:acknowledged` (a head would lag or never\nappear on a lossy mesh, and an acknowledgement is not retrievability);\ndeposit only on `yin.link/publish` (it is not the HEAD the owner named,\nand a reader's `q` over the followed index would lag the names it sees).\n\n### 5.5 Reader behaviour (decision 5)\n\n**Following.** A reader follows a principal by composition:\n`--dht-follow <64 hex>` or `:follow` to the plain API. Every followed\nprincipal must also be declared (`--dht-principal`, 7.1); a follow of an\nundeclared principal refuses startup. One trust root: the declaration.\n\n**Sources.** For each followed principal the reader attaches a reflection\nof that principal's board through every configured peer (`--dht-peer`),\nor through the descriptors the plain API is given. A peer that does not\nserve the name answers `not-found`, and is asked again at the poll\ninterval: it may begin to.\n\n**The rule for one observed trace**, a pure function of the trace, the\nfollowed set, and the principal's floor (the greater of its installed\nsequence and its wanted sequence, 0 when it has neither):\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Case</th><th>Outcome</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Not the closed shape of 5.2</td>\n<td><code>:yin.head/malformed</code>, dropped</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Principal is not the board's</td>\n<td><code>:yin.head/wrong-principal</code>, dropped</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Signature does not verify</td>\n<td><code>:yin.head/bad-proof</code>, dropped</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sequence below the floor</td>\n<td><code>:yin.head/stale</code>, dropped</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sequence at the floor, same manifest</td>\n<td>a duplicate; nothing</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sequence at the floor, another manifest</td>\n<td><code>:yin.head/equivocation</code> naming both; nothing changes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td>Sequence above the floor</td>\n<td>it becomes the principal's <strong>wanted</strong> head</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n- Traces from all sources go through the one rule. The greatest verified\n  sequence wins, whichever source carried it and in whatever order.\n- **Rollback and replay.** An old, validly signed trace re-served by a\n  relay is `:stale`: the floor is the last installed trace, and it is\n  persisted (5.7). Section 9 states the one case the floor cannot cover.\n\n**On a wanted head.** The follower starts `dao.space.dht/load-index` of\nits manifest. A newer wanted head replaces an older one that has not\nloaded; the older load is forgotten when it ends.\n\n- **Install after load.** When the load is `:loaded` and the sequence\n  check of 5.2 passes, the head is installed: it replaces the principal's\n  previous head in the snapshot set, the previous head's load is\n  forgotten, and one `:installed` event is emitted. The swap is one step\n  of the node; no fold ever sees both or neither.\n- The reader's own HEAD is never written by following.\n- A load that fails keeps the previous head installed and emits\n  `:yin.head/unloadable` with the load's failure as data. The trace stays\n  wanted. The follower starts the load again after a delay counted in the\n  node's ticks, doubling from `:repair-ticks` to `:repair-max-ticks`, the\n  bounds automatic repair already has. No deadline ends it; a newer head\n  replaces it.\n\n**The snapshot set** (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 7.2, amended) is: the\nmanifest the directory's HEAD names; every index whose load is `:loaded`;\nand so, with no further rule, the installed head of each followed\nprincipal. Resolution (7.3) is unchanged: one fold over that set under\nthe declared principals.\n\n**A silent publisher.** The reader keeps the installed head, its index and\neverything linked. Nothing lapses and nothing is retracted. The follower\nrecords, as data, the node tick reading at which each source last\nanswered; a drive that wants to judge liveness composes a `dao.lease`\njudge over it (`dao.lease.md`). Nothing in the follower or the linker\nreads that datum.\n\n### 5.6 When `(require 'alib)` refreshes (decision 6)\n\n**On a head event.** A require never asks the network for a newer head.\n\n- An installed head changes the snapshot set, and so the name\n  environment, at that step. A `require` that links resolves against the\n  set as it stands when it runs.\n- A require does not wait for a head that might exist: there is no bound\n  on that wait. The one wait is first contact, below.\n- **Consequence, stated plainly: a name may change meaning between two\n  requires.** A program that requires `alib` in two sessions, or in one\n  session before and after `(reset)`, may link two different modules.\n  The require's provenance carries the snapshot vector, so which index\n  bound the name is always data.\n- **Second consequence: dependency mismatches surface sooner.** When\n  `base` is republished and a reader follows both `base` and `app`,\n  `app`'s pinned address no longer matches and its require refuses\n  `:yin.link.dht/dependency-binding` with `:mismatch` (7.4), until `app`\n  is republished. Following does not create this; it stops hiding it.\n- **First contact.** A require whose name is `:absent` while some\n  followed principal has no installed head parks pending on that\n  principal, as a require under a DHT link source already may (R10). It\n  is re-checked when a head of a principal it waits on is installed\n  (R11's shape, a new event kind). `(abandon)` and the link policy end it\n  as they end any pending run.\n- **On demand.** `(yin.head/heads)` answers what is installed, wanted and\n  refused, per principal.\n\n**Pinning.** `--dht-manifest <address>` is the escape hatch, redefined:\nthis run loads that index as a snapshot before evaluation is admitted,\nfollows no head, and writes no head record. It is one-shot and never\nsaved. A reproducible run names its index every time it starts. A direct\nentry (`--link-name my.lib=<address>`) still pins one name and still wins\nover the fold.\n\nRejected: fetch the head on every require (a require would wait on a\npublisher that owes no answer); refresh only on demand (it is not\n\"always\").\n\n### 5.7 Persistence and relink (decision 7)\n\n**Persistence.** The follower's durable state is the last installed\ntrace of each followed principal, verbatim, in `<node dir>/heads.edn`:\n\n```clojure\n{:version 1\n :heads {\"ed25519:<hex>\" {:yin.head/envelope {...} :yin.head/proof {...}}}}\n```\n\n- It is evidence, not a conclusion: the floor, the manifest and the\n  proof a relay re-serves are all read from it.\n- It is replaced atomically by the node's owner in the tick that reports\n  `:installed`. A crash between the swap and the write resumes at the\n  previous head and installs the newer one again; install is idempotent.\n- At startup each record is verified again, its principal checked against\n  the followed set, and its index loaded from the local store. The blobs\n  are local, so the load fetches nothing. A record that fails\n  verification refuses startup naming it; nothing falls back to no floor.\n- A record for a principal no longer followed is dropped at the next\n  write.\n- An index loaded by hand, `(dao.space.dht/load-index m)`, stays process\n  state. It is a pin made at the prompt, and a restart forgets it.\n\nRejected: recording observed traces as datoms in the reader's own index.\nThe write would move the reader's HEAD; two nodes that follow each other\nwould then move each other's heads without end.\n\n**Relink: a session keeps what it linked. `(reset)` is the way to the\nnew module.**\n\n- A linked module is never replaced under a live session. Its manifest\n  address is in the session's module registry, other modules are\n  discharged against that address (7.4), and code already running closed\n  over it.\n- `yin.vm` learns nothing. The repeat `(require 'alib)` of a linked name\n  stays what it is today, at the VM, with no consultation of any head.\n- **The change is reported, never silent.** When an installed head makes\n  a name linked in the session resolve to another address, the shell\n  prints one line: the name, the linked address, the resolved address,\n  the principal and sequence, and that `(reset)` then `(require ...)`\n  links it. The set is derived, the registry's `{name address}` against\n  the fold, and stored nowhere. `(yin.head/moved)` answers it on demand.\n\nRejected: relink in place (needs module unload and a rule for\ncontinuations captured against the old address; neither exists);\nrefusing the repeat require (it would put head awareness in the VM's\nrequire path, and fail a program for what a remote party did).\n\n### 5.8 Command line and saved state (decision 8)\n\n- **The token** is `yin:<host:port>/<principal>`. The publisher prints it\n  once, when its socket binds, not after each publication: it no longer\n  changes. A three-part token is refused with the message that a manifest\n  is a pin and belongs to `--dht-manifest`.\n- **`dht join <token>`** becomes `--index-store dht:<dir> --dht-peer\n  <host:port> --dht-principal <hex> --dht-follow <hex>`.\n- **Its first manifest** comes from the first trace it honours, by 5.5.\n  The shell admits evaluation at once and says, per followed principal,\n  whether a head is installed. It does not block the prompt on a\n  publisher: 5.6 parks the require instead.\n- **`--dht-manifest` with a saved `--dht-follow`**: the pin wins for that\n  run. The banner says following is suspended and `heads.edn` is neither\n  read as a snapshot nor written.\n- **`state.edn`** keeps flags, as now. It gains `--dht-follow`\n  (repeatable). It never holds a head; `heads.edn` does.\n  `--dht-manifest` stays one-shot.\n- **Hydration into HEAD is removed** (D1): `refused-head`, `hydrated`\n  and the `::hydrating` admission gate go. A pinned index that cannot\n  load still refuses the shell, as now.\n- The banner of a publishing node with a key states that its head is\n  served to any peer that asks its address, and the banner of a following\n  node names whom it follows and that it re-serves their heads when it\n  publishes.\n\n### 5.9 Failure and security (decision 9)\n\nSection 9 is the table. The rules it rests on:\n\n- A trace is honoured only when it verifies under a key the reader\n  declared. Who served it is never evidence.\n- A head is installed only after its whole index is local and its\n  sequence is confirmed by that index.\n- The floor never moves down and survives a restart.\n- No failure retracts an installed head, and none waits on a clock.\n\n### 5.10 Portability (decision 10)\n\n**Portable `.cljc`, identical on the JVM, Node and Dart:** the trace, its\nsigned bytes and the rule of 5.5 (`yin.vm.linker.head`); the board and\nthe follower's step; the node's mirror and reflections\n(`dao.stream.remote`, `dao.stream.udp`, both portable today); the\nrendering of `heads.edn`.\n\n**Host seams, all existing:** Ed25519 (`yin.vm.linker.sign`); the\ndatagram bind (`dao.stream.datagram.*`); the atomic file replace\n(`dao.space.store.fs`). No new seam is needed.\n\n**A browser** has no UDP and no Ed25519 primitive here: it follows\nnothing, as it resolves no signed name (6.4). A WebSocket source is the\nroute when signed names reach a browser build.\n\n**ClojureDart traps to design against:**\n\n- `heads.edn` is rendered with no whitespace before a closing bracket and\n  read back by test on Dart; its reader refuses `{:k 1 }`.\n- Host branches are written `#?(:cljd ... :clj ... :cljs ...)` with\n  `:cljd` first; `:clj` alone does not exclude the Dart build.\n- A protocol or reify method never takes `[_ _]`; a second ignored\n  parameter is named.\n- No test reaches a private var by `#'ns/name` across namespaces; a\n  helper a test needs is public.\n- The sequence is an index `t`. It stays within 2^53 so the same integer\n  is exact on Node, and the vectors include a sequence above 2^32.\n- A manifest address decoded from the wire is compared with `=`, never\n  by identity: ClojureScript keywords are not interned across reads.\n- The proof is verified over `dao.jing/canonical-bytes` of the envelope\n  as decoded from the channel codec (`dao.stream.cbor`), a different\n  encoder. H0 asserts on all three hosts that a trace sent through the\n  channel codec verifies.\n\n## 6. The plain Clojure API\n\nThe one path, for any Clojure program:\n\n```text\njoin -> publish! -> assert! -> announce! -> deposit!       (publisher)\njoin -> follow -> step ... -> names -> load-module -> link (reader)\n```\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Function</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>\n<tr><td><code>(yin.vm.linker.head/trace key manifest seq)</code></td>\n<td>The signed trace of 5.2.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>(head/verify trace)</code></td>\n<td>True or false; never throws.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>(head/seq-of datoms)</code></td>\n<td>The greatest <code>t</code>: the sequence of the index holding them.\n</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>(head/judge floor followed trace)</code></td>\n<td>The rule of 5.5: <code>:wanted</code>, <code>:duplicate</code> or a\nrefusal, as data.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>(head/board-name principal)</code></td>\n<td>The table key of 5.1.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>(head/deposit! node key manifest seq)</code></td>\n<td>Sign and append to the node's own board; refuses\n<code>:yin.head/seq-collision</code>, <code>:yin.head/no-key</code>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td><code>(head/follow node opts)</code></td>\n<td>Compose a follower over the node: <code>:follow</code> principals,\n<code>:sources</code> descriptors (default: the node's peers),\n<code>:heads</code> the persisted records.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>(head/step follower node)</code></td>\n<td>One pass, called by the node's one owner after\n<code>dao.space.dht/step</code>: answers\n<code>[follower node events]</code>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>(head/heads follower)</code></td>\n<td>Per principal: <code>:installed</code>, <code>:wanted</code>, the\nlast refusal, each source's last answer reading. The value\n<code>heads.edn</code> is rendered from.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>(head/moved names registry)</code></td>\n<td>The linked names whose resolved address differs (5.7).</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n`dao.space.dht` gains two payload-agnostic things and learns nothing\nabout heads: a `:table` option to `join` (a mirror table the node's step\nanswers on its socket, bounded as section 7 says), and\n`(dao.space.dht/reflect node descriptor)`, a reflection through that same\nsocket.\n\nEvents are plain maps under `:yin.head/event`: `:installed` (principal,\nsequence, manifest, the previous manifest), `:refused` (a reason of 5.5\nor `:yin.head/seq-mismatch`, with the trace), and `:unloadable` (the\nload's failure).\n\n`yin.repl` adds a `yin.head` host module, `heads` and `moved`, each\nargument checking and one call into the functions above\n(`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` section 10, the interface gate). The shell owns\nonly the file, the lines and the flags.\n\n## 7. Limits and defaults\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Datum</th><th>Default</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Board capacity</td><td>1</td><td>Fixed, not an option (5.3).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td><code>:head-poll-ticks</code></td><td>5000</td>\n<td>Node ticks between two <code>next</code> requests to one source for\none principal. The bound on how late a head is observed.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>:dao.stream.remote/resend-after</code></td><td>2</td>\n<td>The link's resend rule on UDP (<code>dao.stream.remote.md</code>\n2.5).</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>:max-attachments</code></td><td>64</td>\n<td>Remote addresses the node's mirror holds channel state for;\nevict-oldest. An evicted asker's next request re-creates it.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Head load retry</td>\n<td><code>:repair-ticks</code> to <code>:repair-max-ticks</code></td>\n<td>Doubling, as repair (5.5).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Followed principals</td><td>at most 64</td>\n<td>A composition bound; more refuses startup.</td></tr>\n</table>\n\nFollowing never makes the node `busy?`. The owner's idle cadence must not\nexceed `:head-poll-ticks`.\n\n## 8. What is rejected\n\n- **X1. The head under a key in the DHT** (a signed mutable record, as\n  BitTorrent's BEP 44). It survives the publisher going offline with no\n  relay configured, and that is its whole merit. Against it: it gives\n  `dao.jing.dht` roots, record validation, a sequence comparison and an\n  overwrite rule, each of which section 1 of that contract excludes and\n  each of which is semantics in the syntax layer; it is the\n  mutable-pointer service the owner's second sentence sets aside; and it\n  changes wire v1. A relay gives the same availability with no new\n  mechanism.\n- **X2. Both media.** Two paths to one fact need a rule for when they\n  disagree. The sequence already is that rule, so the second medium adds\n  cost and no property.\n- **X3. A hash chain (`:prev`).** It would let a reader audit a\n  publisher's history of heads. Nothing here needs that history, the\n  chain is not derivable from the index, and a capacity-1 ring could not\n  serve its links.\n- **X4. The signature in `m`.** C1.\n- **X5. Hydrating the followed head into the reader's HEAD.** D1.\n- **X6. A head operation on `dao.stream.apply` or an rpc.** It would put\n  a transport concept behind an apply word (owner, 2026-09-29) and make\n  the head a service. A ring served as itself needs neither.\n- **X7. A new DHT wire op (`:head`).** X1's objections, plus a version\n  break for a datum the socket's other interpreter already carries.\n- **X8. Push.** `dao.stream.remote.md` section 1 lists readiness\n  notification as a non-goal. The follower polls at a bounded cadence.\n\n## 9. Failure modes\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Event</th><th>What the reader does</th><th>What it cannot do</th>\n</tr>\n<tr><td>A forged trace (no key)</td>\n<td><code>:yin.head/bad-proof</code>; dropped.</td><td>-</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A trace for an undeclared principal</td>\n<td>Never asked for: only followed, declared principals have a\nreflection. One arriving on a board is\n<code>:yin.head/wrong-principal</code>.</td><td>-</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A stale trace re-served (replay)</td>\n<td><code>:yin.head/stale</code> against the persisted floor.</td>\n<td><strong>First contact has no floor.</strong> A relay may hand a new\nreader an old, valid head, and the reader installs it. It asks every\nsource and keeps the greatest sequence; the publisher's own address in\nthe token is the mitigation. No trace says how new it is.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A publisher that rewinds (signs an older index)</td>\n<td>The older index has a lower <code>t</code>: <code>:stale</code>. A\nhigher sequence on an old index is <code>:yin.head/seq-mismatch</code>\nafter the load.</td>\n<td>A key holder can build a new index with a higher <code>t</code> that\nomits anything. The reader trusts a declared principal for its own head;\nthat is what declaring means.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Two manifests at one sequence</td>\n<td><code>:yin.head/equivocation</code>; the installed head stays.</td>\n<td>It cannot tell which the publisher meant. A greater sequence ends\nit.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A relay that withholds</td>\n<td>Nothing arrives from it; other sources are unaffected.</td>\n<td><strong>Withholding is undetectable</strong> when every source\nwithholds: silence and \"no new head\" look the same, and no timeout may\ntell them apart here.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A trace naming a manifest nobody holds</td>\n<td><code>:yin.head/unloadable</code>; the previous head stays; retry\nat the bounded delay; a relay never re-serves it, having not installed\nit.</td><td>-</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The publisher's directory is lost, the key kept</td>\n<td>New traces are <code>:stale</code>.</td>\n<td>Recover without the publisher's remedy of 5.2, short of removing\nthe principal's record from <code>heads.edn</code> by hand.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The key is disclosed</td>\n<td>Whoever holds it moves the head. Remove the declaration\n(6.5).</td><td>-</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A source restarts</td>\n<td><code>cursor-mismatch</code>, then a fresh <code>:oldest</code>.</td>\n<td>-</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Spoofed askers on the publisher's socket</td>\n<td>Channel state is bounded by <code>:max-attachments</code>.</td>\n<td><strong>An answer is larger than its request</strong> (a trace is\nabout 300 bytes) and goes to an unproven source address. The DHT's cookie\ngate does not cover <code>dao.stream.udp</code>. Section 11 defers the\nreturn-path proof; H1 measures the ratio and states it.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Anyone reads the board</td>\n<td>By design. A principal's head is public where its node is reachable,\nas its index already is under <code>--dht-publish</code>.</td>\n<td>Restrict readers, until a gate is composed on the entry\n(<code>dao.stream.remote.md</code> section 7).</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n## 10. Implementation slices\n\nOrder: **H0 -> H1 -> H2 -> H3 -> H4.** Each lands alone with the tree\ngreen on the JVM, Node and Dart. H0 and H1 own disjoint files.\n\n**H0 -- the trace and its rule.** Pure, host neutral. Files: new\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head.cljc` (`trace`, `verify`, `seq-of`, `judge`,\n`board-name`), `src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/sign.cljc` (the head domain\nprefix), `test/yin/vm/linker/sign_vectors.edn`, new\n`test/yin/vm/linker/head_test.cljc`. Complete when:\n- One trace under RFC 8032 TEST 1's seed is in the vectors with its\n  canonical bytes, signed message and signature, asserted byte for byte\n  on all three hosts; one has a sequence above 2^32.\n- Tampering with the principal, manifest or sequence, one flipped\n  signature bit, another key, an extra key in either map, and a\n  name-envelope signature presented as a head proof each fail.\n- A trace encoded and decoded by `dao.stream.cbor` verifies on each host.\n- `judge` produces each row of the 5.5 table, and its answer over a set\n  of traces is the same in every arrival order, equivocation excepted and\n  asserted separately.\n- `seq-of` over a rehydrated index equals `seq-of` before the restart,\n  and a further round raises it. Two HEAD writes of one directory never\n  share it, across rounds, name transactions and the unwritten-row\n  recovery of `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 5.1. **If any HEAD move is found\n  that commits no transaction, this slice stops and reports it: the\n  derived sequence does not hold and section 5.2 is reopened.**\n\n**H1 -- the node serves and reflects streams.** Files:\n`src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc` (`:table`, `reflect`, the port and mirror\nin `step`), `test/dao/space/dht_test.cljc`, `test/dao/jing/dht/mesh.cljc`\n(carry non-DHT datagrams), `docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md` (status\nline; one sentence in section 5 that a table key may be a conventional\nname whose ring identity is learned from its cursors),\n`docs/design/dao.stream.datagram.md` section 6 (the node as a composition\nof both interpreters). Nothing here names a head. Complete when:\n- Two mesh nodes: B reflects a capacity-1 ring in A's table and reads\n  its value; a second deposit is read; three deposits between reads give\n  `gap` then the newest.\n- A restarted A answers B's old cursor `cursor-mismatch`; an absent name\n  answers `not-found`; a writer op on the `#{:reader}` entry answers\n  `no-surface`.\n- The DHT's existing mesh tests pass unchanged with the table in place,\n  and neither interpreter produces a fact or an answer for the other's\n  datagrams.\n- A solo node opens no socket and serves no table.\n- With more than `:max-attachments` asking addresses, the node's channel\n  state never exceeds the bound and an evicted asker is answered again.\n- The slice records the measured request and answer sizes of one `next`.\n- Time advances only by appended ticks.\n\n**H2 -- deposit and follow, plain Clojure.** Files:\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head.cljc` (`deposit!`, `follow`, `step`,\n`heads`, `moved`), `src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/dht.cljc` (`snapshots` reads\nfollowed heads only through loaded indexes; no change if 5.5 holds),\nnew `test/yin/vm/linker/head_follow_test.cljc`,\n`docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md` (1, 7.2, 10, 13: D2). Complete when:\n- A publishes `alib`, deposits; B, given A's address and principal and\n  no manifest, installs A's head and resolves `alib`. A republishes; B\n  resolves the new address after one poll, with no call from the test\n  but ticks.\n- Ten deposits between two polls install one head, the last.\n- A relay R following A re-serves the installed trace; with A closed, a\n  new reader C given only R installs it. R with `:publish?` false serves\n  no board.\n- R re-serving an older valid trace to B is `:stale`; to a first-contact\n  reader also given A it loses to A's greater sequence; given R alone it\n  is installed, and the test asserts that as the stated limit.\n- A trace whose sequence exceeds its index's greatest `t` is loaded,\n  refused `:yin.head/seq-mismatch`, and the previous head stays.\n- A trace whose manifest no peer holds is `:unloadable`; the previous\n  head stays; the load is started again only after the delay; a newer\n  loadable head replaces it at once; R never serves the unloadable one.\n- B closed and reopened over the same directory with its `heads` value\n  restored resolves `alib` **with zero `:jing/get` on the request ring**,\n  and refuses the older trace as `:stale`.\n- Two nodes following each other install each other's heads and reach\n  quiescence: neither HEAD moves.\n- The previous head's load is forgotten on install; the snapshot vector\n  never holds two heads of one followed principal.\n- `moved` names exactly the linked names whose address changed.\n\n**H3 -- the REPL.** Files: `src/cljc/yin/repl/dht.cljc`,\n`src/cljc/yin/repl/main.cljc`, `src/cljc/yin/repl/state.cljc`,\n`src/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc` (the `yin.head` host module),\n`src/cljc/yin/repl/link.cljc` and `src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc` (the\nfirst-contact pending), the tests beside each. Complete when:\n- The token prints once at bind as `yin:<host:port>/<principal>`;\n  `parse-token` refuses the three-part form with the pin message.\n- `dht join <token>` saves `--dht-follow`; a bare restart follows again\n  and resolves from `heads.edn` before any datagram is answered.\n- `--dht-follow` of an undeclared principal refuses startup.\n- A `(require 'alib)` typed before the first head parks, and completes\n  on the head's install with no typed line; `(abandon)` ends it; an\n  unrelated node event leaves `:checks` unchanged.\n- An installed head that moves a linked name prints the one line of 5.7;\n  the repeat require is unchanged; `(reset)` then `require` links the new\n  address.\n- `heads.edn` cases: absent, malformed, a record that fails verification,\n  a record for an unfollowed principal. Each is read on Dart.\n- The host module's functions hold no rule: a reviewer refuses any fold,\n  verification or file write in `yin.repl.query`.\n\n**H4 -- the pin, the removal of hydration, end to end.** Files:\n`src/cljc/yin/repl/dht.cljc`, `src/cljc/yin/repl/main.cljc`,\n`test/yin/repl/dht_process_test.clj`,\n`test/yin/vm/linker/dht_end_to_end_test.cljc`, `docs/design/` for\n`yin.repl` and `dao.jing.dht.md` section 1 (the pointer here). Complete\nwhen:\n- `--dht-manifest` loads a snapshot, follows nothing and leaves HEAD and\n  `heads.edn` untouched; with a saved follow, the banner says following\n  is suspended. `refused-head`, `hydrated` and `::hydrating` are gone.\n- Process A publishes `alib`; process B joins with the two-part token\n  and requires it on each of the four VMs, on the JVM and on Node. A\n  republishes; B, after `(reset)`, links the new module. B restarted\n  with A stopped still requires it.\n- A process pinned with `--dht-manifest` links the first module after A\n  republished.\n- The in-process form passes on Dart over the mesh seam.\n- The full three-host suite is run once, at landing.\n\n## 11. Deferred\n\n- **Finding sources.** A reader asks the peers it was given. Rendezvous,\n  and asking peers the DHT's routing table discovered, are\n  `dao.stream.discovery.md`'s.\n- **A return-path proof for `dao.stream.udp`** (the cookie gate the DHT\n  has), and with it the removal of the amplification row of section 9.\n- **Gating the board** by a ShiBi decision on the table entry.\n- **Liveness.** A `dao.lease` composition over the follower's\n  last-answer readings, for a drive that wants \"this publisher is gone\".\n- **Relink in place**, with module unload and the fate of continuations\n  captured against the old address.\n- **Resolving a transitive require by its pinned address**\n  (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` section 13). Following makes its absence felt\n  (5.6); it is the fix for the mismatch, and it is not designed here.\n- **Collecting superseded indexes.** A forgotten head's blobs stay in the\n  content log; `dao.jing.dht.md` defers garbage collection.\n- **Key rotation and revocation** (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` section 13). A\n  rotation would move a head board's name.\n- **A head history**, should an audit ever need one (X3).\n- **Persisting loads made by hand.**\n- **Push**, if `dao.stream.remote` ever gains readiness.\n- **A browser follower** over a WebSocket source.\n\n## 12. Questions only the owner can answer\n\n1. **Does \"always\" reach into a live session?** This design keeps a\n   session on the module it linked, reports the move, and links the new\n   one after `(reset)` (5.7). If a repeat `(require 'alib)` must switch a\n   running session to the new module, relink in place leaves section 11\n   and needs its own design first.\n2. **What should a bare `q` see after `dht join`?** Today it answers the\n   publisher's facts, because hydration made them the reader's own index.\n   Here the reader's index is its own, and the publisher's is reached\n   with `(dao.space.dht/q <manifest> ...)`, the manifest from\n   `(yin.head/heads)`. If `q` should span both, that is an explicit union\n   of two sources at the query, and a decision about its default.\n3. **May a publishing node re-serve the heads it follows by default?** It\n   makes every publishing follower a relay, which is what keeps a head\n   reachable while its publisher is offline. It also tells anyone who\n   asks that node whom it follows.\n4. **Is every HEAD move the head you mean?** HEAD moves on every round\n   that adds rows, so a follower loads a new index for each round the\n   publisher evaluates, not only for each `yin.link/publish`. The design\n   follows your words. The alternative, a head only at each publish, is\n   quieter and is no longer \"whatever the HEAD is\".\n\n## Lineage\n\nThe tuple space made stigmergy precise; this makes one trace of it\ndurable enough to follow. `announce!` was already the deposit, kept\nprivate. A head board is the same act with an author and an audience: a\nsigned value on a ring one agent writes and any agent may read, carried\nby the mechanism that carries any stream. Datomic's connection hands a\npeer the newest database value; here no connection exists, only the\ntrace, and each reader decides whose traces are worth following.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
