{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md | sed -n '160,540p'\"","aggregated_output":"   160\t- The table key is the conventional name\n   161\t  `\"yin.head/ed25519:<64 lowercase hex>\"`: the string `\"yin.head/\"`\n   162\t  followed by the principal id of `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 6.2. The ring\n   163\t  keeps its own per-process identity, which rides its cursors.\n   164\t- The channel is `dao.stream.udp` on the node's one socket.\n   165\t  `dao.stream.datagram.md` section 6 already lets `dao.jing.dht` and\n   166\t  `dao.stream.udp` share it: the DHT owns the maps carrying\n   167\t  `:dao.jing.dht/v`, the UDP channel every other decodable value. One\n   168\t  address, one NAT mapping, both interpreters, neither aware of the other.\n   169\t- So the token `yin:<host:port>/<principal>` is exactly a remote\n   170\t  descriptor:\n   171\t\n   172\t  ```clojure\n   173\t  {:dao.stream/type     :dao.stream/remote\n   174\t   :dao.stream/identity \"yin.head/ed25519:<hex>\"\n   175\t   :dao.stream/channel  {:dao.stream/type     :dao.stream/udp\n   176\t                         :dao.stream/identity <channel identity>\n   177\t                         :dao.stream.udp/host h\n   178\t                         :dao.stream.udp/port p}}\n   179\t  ```\n   180\t\n   181\t- Any other channel `dao.stream.remote.md` section 3 defines (WebSocket,\n   182\t  a relay pair) carries the same entry unchanged. The plain API takes\n   183\t  descriptors; only the token form presumes UDP.\n   184\t- **Nothing is put in the DHT.** The DHT is used as today, to fetch the\n   185\t  blobs of the index the trace names.\n   186\t\n   187\t**A relay may serve the trace, and needs no trust.** A trace is\n   188\tself-authenticating (section 5.2). Any node that follows a principal, has\n   189\ta socket and is publishing (`:publish?`) enters its own capacity-1 ring\n   190\tunder the same name, holding the last trace it **installed**, verbatim. It\n   191\tis that node's stream, written by that node; the authorship of the value\n   192\tinside is the signature's. A fetch-only node serves nothing, as its banner\n   193\talready promises. No node is a relay by kind: a relay is a follower with a\n   194\tsocket.\n   195\t\n   196\t**Availability.**\n   197\t\n   198\t- Publisher online and reachable: a reader observes a new head within one\n   199\t  poll interval (section 7).\n   200\t- Publisher offline, a relay reachable: the reader observes the last head\n   201\t  that relay installed. It can load that head's index only as far as the\n   202\t  DHT still holds the blobs (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 5.5.6).\n   203\t- Publisher offline, no relay: the reader observes nothing new. It keeps\n   204\t  the head it persisted and everything it linked. Nothing times out.\n   205\t- A reader that has never had contact and reaches no source has no head.\n   206\t  Section 5.6 says what a `require` does then.\n   207\t\n   208\tRejected: a signed mutable record under a DHT key (section 8, X1); both\n   209\tmedia at once (X2).\n   210\t\n   211\t### 5.2 Trace schema (decision 2)\n   212\t\n   213\tA trace is one plain value: an envelope and its proof, the shape a name\n   214\tassertion already has (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 6.1, 6.3).\n   215\t\n   216\t```clojure\n   217\t{:yin.head/envelope {:yin.head/principal \"ed25519:<64 lowercase hex>\"\n   218\t                     :yin.head/manifest  :segment/...\n   219\t                     :yin.head/seq       1042}\n   220\t :yin.head/proof    {:yin.head/signature \"<128 lowercase hex>\"}}\n   221\t```\n   222\t\n   223\t- The key sets are closed. No other key is written, and a trace carrying\n   224\t  one is malformed.\n   225\t- No time is written: the publisher owns no clock. No address is written:\n   226\t  a reply goes to the observed source (`dao.jing.dht.md` section 6), and a\n   227\t  relay serves from its own.\n   228\t- The signed message is exactly\n   229\t  `UTF-8(\"yin.head/trace:v1\\n\") || dao.jing/canonical-bytes(envelope)`.\n   230\t  The prefix is domain separation: a name-envelope signature is never a\n   231\t  head proof, and the reverse.\n   232\t- Signing and verification are `yin.vm.linker.sign`'s Ed25519 primitive,\n   233\t  unchanged, with the prefix as the only new datum. The principal is its\n   234\t  own key, so verification needs nothing but the trace.\n   235\t- Verification refuses, as `:yin.head/bad-proof`: a signature that does\n   236\t  not verify, a malformed key or signature, and a principal that is not\n   237\t  `\"ed25519:\"` plus a well-formed key.\n   238\t\n   239\t**The sequence is derived, on both sides.**\n   240\t\n   241\t- `:yin.head/seq` is the greatest transaction `t` among the datoms of the\n   242\t  index the manifest names. The publisher reads it from the round it just\n   243\t  committed. It stores no counter and no trace.\n   244\t- It survives a restart because the index does: `yin.repl.index/rehydrate`\n   245\t  preserves each datom's `t`, and the transactor derives the next `t` from\n   246\t  the restored log.\n   247\t- A reader verifies it. After the index is loaded, a trace whose sequence\n   248\t  is not the greatest `t` of the loaded datoms is refused\n   249\t  `:yin.head/seq-mismatch` and is never installed. The sequence orders\n   250\t  traces before the load; the index confirms it after.\n   251\t- A publisher never signs a second manifest at a sequence it has signed\n   252\t  in this process: the deposit is refused locally,\n   253\t  `:yin.head/seq-collision`. Slice H0 pins by test that two HEAD writes\n   254\t  of one directory never share a greatest `t`.\n   255\t- **The key kept and the directory lost** restarts `t`, and with it the\n   256\t  sequence, exactly as `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 6.5 says of the name\n   257\t  sequence. Readers refuse the new traces as stale. The remedy is that\n   258\t  section's: hydrate from the last published manifest before publishing,\n   259\t  or take a new key.\n   260\t\n   261\tRejected: the `m` slot (C1); a stored counter beside the key; a `:prev`\n   262\thash chain (section 8, X3).\n   263\t\n   264\t### 5.3 Retention (decision 3)\n   265\t\n   266\t**Capacity 1. The latest head wins and the ring itself coalesces.**\n   267\t\n   268\t- No reader needs a superseded head. Loading one is waste, and a head\n   269\t  history is the index's own history, already content-addressed.\n   270\t- **First contact.** The reader mints `:dao.stream/oldest`. On a\n   271\t  capacity-1 ring the earliest retained position is the current head, so\n   272\t  the first `next` answers it. A ring nothing was deposited on answers\n   273\t  `blocked`.\n   274\t- **A slow reader** holding a cursor across two deposits is answered\n   275\t  `gap` with the recovery cursor, which is the current head's position.\n   276\t  It adopts it. The gap is the honest statement \"heads were skipped\", and\n   277\t  it costs nothing: skipping them is the policy.\n   278\t- **A restarted source** has a new ring. The reader's old cursor is\n   279\t  answered `cursor-mismatch`, a present answer; the reader mints\n   280\t  `:oldest` again. The same holds when a peer's entry disappears\n   281\t  (`not-found`) and returns.\n   282\t- A reader keeps one cursor per source and per principal. Cursors never\n   283\t  cross sources.\n   284\t\n   285\tRejected: a larger ring (nothing reads the extra elements); a\n   286\tcomplete-history log on a network-reachable entry (the wrong declared\n   287\tnature for a latest-wins value, `dao.stream.md`, Retention and Gaps).\n   288\t\n   289\t### 5.4 Publisher behaviour (decision 4)\n   290\t\n   291\t- **When.** One trace is deposited at every HEAD move: after the HEAD\n   292\t  write, in the same call, before any network result. This is the rule\n   293\t  HEAD itself follows (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 5.5.6), and it is what the\n   294\t  owner asked for: the head, whatever it is. The deposit is a local\n   295\t  append; no round waits.\n   296\t- **Coalescing is the medium's.** A publisher does not batch. Ten rounds\n   297\t  between two polls leave one trace.\n   298\t- **At startup** a node with a key and a recovered HEAD deposits the trace\n   299\t  of that HEAD at once: manifest from HEAD, sequence from the recovered\n   300\t  datoms, signature from the key. A reader's first contact after the\n   301\t  publisher restarts therefore observes the head without any new round.\n   302\t- **Who deposits.** A node deposits when it has a key, a socket and\n   303\t  `:publish?`. Without a key it signs nothing, as it publishes no name.\n   304\t  Without `:publish?` it serves no board: a head whose blobs it will not\n   305\t  serve is a promise it cannot keep.\n   306\t- **Relation to `announce!` and the ledger ring: beside, not reuse, wrap\n   307\t  or replace.** The ledger ring is the node's private work queue: every\n   308\t  put address and each window close, read once by `step`, for replication\n   309\t  accounting. It is unsigned because `dao.space.dht` holds no key and\n   310\t  knows nothing of `yin.*`, and both are right. Serving it would publish\n   311\t  every put address and still authenticate nothing. The head board is a\n   312\t  second ring with one job. The composition that calls `announce!` calls\n   313\t  the deposit beside it, in that order: `yin.repl.dht`'s `:head-fn`, and\n   314\t  the plain path `publish! -> assert! -> announce! -> deposit!`.\n   315\t- The publisher's own directory holds nothing new. HEAD stays the one\n   316\t  record of what its head is.\n   317\t\n   318\tRejected: deposit only on `:acknowledged` (a head would lag or never\n   319\tappear on a lossy mesh, and an acknowledgement is not retrievability);\n   320\tdeposit only on `yin.link/publish` (it is not the HEAD the owner named,\n   321\tand a reader's `q` over the followed index would lag the names it sees).\n   322\t\n   323\t### 5.5 Reader behaviour (decision 5)\n   324\t\n   325\t**Following.** A reader follows a principal by composition:\n   326\t`--dht-follow <64 hex>` or `:follow` to the plain API. Every followed\n   327\tprincipal must also be declared (`--dht-principal`, 7.1); a follow of an\n   328\tundeclared principal refuses startup. One trust root: the declaration.\n   329\t\n   330\t**Sources.** For each followed principal the reader attaches a reflection\n   331\tof that principal's board through every configured peer (`--dht-peer`),\n   332\tor through the descriptors the plain API is given. A peer that does not\n   333\tserve the name answers `not-found`, and is asked again at the poll\n   334\tinterval: it may begin to.\n   335\t\n   336\t**The rule for one observed trace**, a pure function of the trace, the\n   337\tfollowed set, and the principal's floor (the greater of its installed\n   338\tsequence and its wanted sequence, 0 when it has neither):\n   339\t\n   340\t<table>\n   341\t<tr><th>Case</th><th>Outcome</th></tr>\n   342\t<tr><td>Not the closed shape of 5.2</td>\n   343\t<td><code>:yin.head/malformed</code>, dropped</td></tr>\n   344\t<tr><td>Principal is not the board's</td>\n   345\t<td><code>:yin.head/wrong-principal</code>, dropped</td></tr>\n   346\t<tr><td>Signature does not verify</td>\n   347\t<td><code>:yin.head/bad-proof</code>, dropped</td></tr>\n   348\t<tr><td>Sequence below the floor</td>\n   349\t<td><code>:yin.head/stale</code>, dropped</td></tr>\n   350\t<tr><td>Sequence at the floor, same manifest</td>\n   351\t<td>a duplicate; nothing</td></tr>\n   352\t<tr><td>Sequence at the floor, another manifest</td>\n   353\t<td><code>:yin.head/equivocation</code> naming both; nothing changes</td>\n   354\t</tr>\n   355\t<tr><td>Sequence above the floor</td>\n   356\t<td>it becomes the principal's <strong>wanted</strong> head</td></tr>\n   357\t</table>\n   358\t\n   359\t- Traces from all sources go through the one rule. The greatest verified\n   360\t  sequence wins, whichever source carried it and in whatever order.\n   361\t- **Rollback and replay.** An old, validly signed trace re-served by a\n   362\t  relay is `:stale`: the floor is the last installed trace, and it is\n   363\t  persisted (5.7). Section 9 states the one case the floor cannot cover.\n   364\t\n   365\t**On a wanted head.** The follower starts `dao.space.dht/load-index` of\n   366\tits manifest. A newer wanted head replaces an older one that has not\n   367\tloaded; the older load is forgotten when it ends.\n   368\t\n   369\t- **Install after load.** When the load is `:loaded` and the sequence\n   370\t  check of 5.2 passes, the head is installed: it replaces the principal's\n   371\t  previous head in the snapshot set, the previous head's load is\n   372\t  forgotten, and one `:installed` event is emitted. The swap is one step\n   373\t  of the node; no fold ever sees both or neither.\n   374\t- The reader's own HEAD is never written by following.\n   375\t- A load that fails keeps the previous head installed and emits\n   376\t  `:yin.head/unloadable` with the load's failure as data. The trace stays\n   377\t  wanted. The follower starts the load again after a delay counted in the\n   378\t  node's ticks, doubling from `:repair-ticks` to `:repair-max-ticks`, the\n   379\t  bounds automatic repair already has. No deadline ends it; a newer head\n   380\t  replaces it.\n   381\t\n   382\t**The snapshot set** (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 7.2, amended) is: the\n   383\tmanifest the directory's HEAD names; every index whose load is `:loaded`;\n   384\tand so, with no further rule, the installed head of each followed\n   385\tprincipal. Resolution (7.3) is unchanged: one fold over that set under\n   386\tthe declared principals.\n   387\t\n   388\t**A silent publisher.** The reader keeps the installed head, its index and\n   389\teverything linked. Nothing lapses and nothing is retracted. The follower\n   390\trecords, as data, the node tick reading at which each source last\n   391\tanswered; a drive that wants to judge liveness composes a `dao.lease`\n   392\tjudge over it (`dao.lease.md`). Nothing in the follower or the linker\n   393\treads that datum.\n   394\t\n   395\t### 5.6 When `(require 'alib)` refreshes (decision 6)\n   396\t\n   397\t**On a head event.** A require never asks the network for a newer head.\n   398\t\n   399\t- An installed head changes the snapshot set, and so the name\n   400\t  environment, at that step. A `require` that links resolves against the\n   401\t  set as it stands when it runs.\n   402\t- A require does not wait for a head that might exist: there is no bound\n   403\t  on that wait. The one wait is first contact, below.\n   404\t- **Consequence, stated plainly: a name may change meaning between two\n   405\t  requires.** A program that requires `alib` in two sessions, or in one\n   406\t  session before and after `(reset)`, may link two different modules.\n   407\t  The require's provenance carries the snapshot vector, so which index\n   408\t  bound the name is always data.\n   409\t- **Second consequence: dependency mismatches surface sooner.** When\n   410\t  `base` is republished and a reader follows both `base` and `app`,\n   411\t  `app`'s pinned address no longer matches and its require refuses\n   412\t  `:yin.link.dht/dependency-binding` with `:mismatch` (7.4), until `app`\n   413\t  is republished. Following does not create this; it stops hiding it.\n   414\t- **First contact.** A require whose name is `:absent` while some\n   415\t  followed principal has no installed head parks pending on that\n   416\t  principal, as a require under a DHT link source already may (R10). It\n   417\t  is re-checked when a head of a principal it waits on is installed\n   418\t  (R11's shape, a new event kind). `(abandon)` and the link policy end it\n   419\t  as they end any pending run.\n   420\t- **On demand.** `(yin.head/heads)` answers what is installed, wanted and\n   421\t  refused, per principal.\n   422\t\n   423\t**Pinning.** `--dht-manifest <address>` is the escape hatch, redefined:\n   424\tthis run loads that index as a snapshot before evaluation is admitted,\n   425\tfollows no head, and writes no head record. It is one-shot and never\n   426\tsaved. A reproducible run names its index every time it starts. A direct\n   427\tentry (`--link-name my.lib=<address>`) still pins one name and still wins\n   428\tover the fold.\n   429\t\n   430\tRejected: fetch the head on every require (a require would wait on a\n   431\tpublisher that owes no answer); refresh only on demand (it is not\n   432\t\"always\").\n   433\t\n   434\t### 5.7 Persistence and relink (decision 7)\n   435\t\n   436\t**Persistence.** The follower's durable state is the last installed\n   437\ttrace of each followed principal, verbatim, in `<node dir>/heads.edn`:\n   438\t\n   439\t```clojure\n   440\t{:version 1\n   441\t :heads {\"ed25519:<hex>\" {:yin.head/envelope {...} :yin.head/proof {...}}}}\n   442\t```\n   443\t\n   444\t- It is evidence, not a conclusion: the floor, the manifest and the\n   445\t  proof a relay re-serves are all read from it.\n   446\t- It is replaced atomically by the node's owner in the tick that reports\n   447\t  `:installed`. A crash between the swap and the write resumes at the\n   448\t  previous head and installs the newer one again; install is idempotent.\n   449\t- At startup each record is verified again, its principal checked against\n   450\t  the followed set, and its index loaded from the local store. The blobs\n   451\t  are local, so the load fetches nothing. A record that fails\n   452\t  verification refuses startup naming it; nothing falls back to no floor.\n   453\t- A record for a principal no longer followed is dropped at the next\n   454\t  write.\n   455\t- An index loaded by hand, `(dao.space.dht/load-index m)`, stays process\n   456\t  state. It is a pin made at the prompt, and a restart forgets it.\n   457\t\n   458\tRejected: recording observed traces as datoms in the reader's own index.\n   459\tThe write would move the reader's HEAD; two nodes that follow each other\n   460\twould then move each other's heads without end.\n   461\t\n   462\t**Relink: a session keeps what it linked. `(reset)` is the way to the\n   463\tnew module.**\n   464\t\n   465\t- A linked module is never replaced under a live session. Its manifest\n   466\t  address is in the session's module registry, other modules are\n   467\t  discharged against that address (7.4), and code already running closed\n   468\t  over it.\n   469\t- `yin.vm` learns nothing. The repeat `(require 'alib)` of a linked name\n   470\t  stays what it is today, at the VM, with no consultation of any head.\n   471\t- **The change is reported, never silent.** When an installed head makes\n   472\t  a name linked in the session resolve to another address, the shell\n   473\t  prints one line: the name, the linked address, the resolved address,\n   474\t  the principal and sequence, and that `(reset)` then `(require ...)`\n   475\t  links it. The set is derived, the registry's `{name address}` against\n   476\t  the fold, and stored nowhere. `(yin.head/moved)` answers it on demand.\n   477\t\n   478\tRejected: relink in place (needs module unload and a rule for\n   479\tcontinuations captured against the old address; neither exists);\n   480\trefusing the repeat require (it would put head awareness in the VM's\n   481\trequire path, and fail a program for what a remote party did).\n   482\t\n   483\t### 5.8 Command line and saved state (decision 8)\n   484\t\n   485\t- **The token** is `yin:<host:port>/<principal>`. The publisher prints it\n   486\t  once, when its socket binds, not after each publication: it no longer\n   487\t  changes. A three-part token is refused with the message that a manifest\n   488\t  is a pin and belongs to `--dht-manifest`.\n   489\t- **`dht join <token>`** becomes `--index-store dht:<dir> --dht-peer\n   490\t  <host:port> --dht-principal <hex> --dht-follow <hex>`.\n   491\t- **Its first manifest** comes from the first trace it honours, by 5.5.\n   492\t  The shell admits evaluation at once and says, per followed principal,\n   493\t  whether a head is installed. It does not block the prompt on a\n   494\t  publisher: 5.6 parks the require instead.\n   495\t- **`--dht-manifest` with a saved `--dht-follow`**: the pin wins for that\n   496\t  run. The banner says following is suspended and `heads.edn` is neither\n   497\t  read as a snapshot nor written.\n   498\t- **`state.edn`** keeps flags, as now. It gains `--dht-follow`\n   499\t  (repeatable). It never holds a head; `heads.edn` does.\n   500\t  `--dht-manifest` stays one-shot.\n   501\t- **Hydration into HEAD is removed** (D1): `refused-head`, `hydrated`\n   502\t  and the `::hydrating` admission gate go. A pinned index that cannot\n   503\t  load still refuses the shell, as now.\n   504\t- The banner of a publishing node with a key states that its head is\n   505\t  served to any peer that asks its address, and the banner of a following\n   506\t  node names whom it follows and that it re-serves their heads when it\n   507\t  publishes.\n   508\t\n   509\t### 5.9 Failure and security (decision 9)\n   510\t\n   511\tSection 9 is the table. The rules it rests on:\n   512\t\n   513\t- A trace is honoured only when it verifies under a key the reader\n   514\t  declared. Who served it is never evidence.\n   515\t- A head is installed only after its whole index is local and its\n   516\t  sequence is confirmed by that index.\n   517\t- The floor never moves down and survives a restart.\n   518\t- No failure retracts an installed head, and none waits on a clock.\n   519\t\n   520\t### 5.10 Portability (decision 10)\n   521\t\n   522\t**Portable `.cljc`, identical on the JVM, Node and Dart:** the trace, its\n   523\tsigned bytes and the rule of 5.5 (`yin.vm.linker.head`); the board and\n   524\tthe follower's step; the node's mirror and reflections\n   525\t(`dao.stream.remote`, `dao.stream.udp`, both portable today); the\n   526\trendering of `heads.edn`.\n   527\t\n   528\t**Host seams, all existing:** Ed25519 (`yin.vm.linker.sign`); the\n   529\tdatagram bind (`dao.stream.datagram.*`); the atomic file replace\n   530\t(`dao.space.store.fs`). No new seam is needed.\n   531\t\n   532\t**A browser** has no UDP and no Ed25519 primitive here: it follows\n   533\tnothing, as it resolves no signed name (6.4). A WebSocket source is the\n   534\troute when signed names reach a browser build.\n   535\t\n   536\t**ClojureDart traps to design against:**\n   537\t\n   538\t- `heads.edn` is rendered with no whitespace before a closing bracket and\n   539\t  read back by test on Dart; its reader refuses `{:k 1 }`.\n   540\t- Host branches are written `#?(:cljd ... :clj ... :cljs ...)` with\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
