{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc | sed -n '1,340p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc | sed -n '1,280p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t(ns dao.stream.remote\n     2\t  \"DaoStream remote: any stream, reachable as itself\n     3\t   (docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md, sections 2 through 2.5).\n     4\t\n     5\t   A peer that holds a handle answers the handle's own operations as\n     6\t   data over a channel; a peer at the other end holds a handle -- the\n     7\t   reflection -- whose operations are those questions asked across the\n     8\t   channel, with the source's outcome maps returned verbatim.\n     9\t\n    10\t   `mirror-step` is the pure answering step a peer runs over a channel\n    11\t   against its table. It never constructs, parses or rewrites a cursor,\n    12\t   an anchor or an outcome, and its steps 1 to 4 are its only contacts\n    13\t   with the entry's handle: every cursor, next and append! runs through\n    14\t   `dao.stream.middleware/apply-request` with the channel context, so\n    15\t   nothing bypasses the entry's middleware or its declared surface.\n    16\t   `attacher` is the composition's :dao.stream/attach entry for\n    17\t   :dao.stream/remote descriptors; it keeps one link per channel\n    18\t   descriptor, shared by every reflection through that channel, and\n    19\t   answers ok at once -- the contract's deferred remote confirmation.\n    20\t   There is no :dao.stream/create entry: attaching through this\n    21\t   transport never creates.\"\n    22\t  (:require [dao.stream :as stream]\n    23\t            [dao.stream.middleware :as middleware]))\n    24\t\n    25\t\n    26\t;; =============================================================================\n    27\t;; Wire shapes (2.1)\n    28\t;; =============================================================================\n    29\t\n    30\t(def ^:private wire-ops\n    31\t  \"The four wire operations. close! never crosses -- the mirror holds\n    32\t   nothing per remote party to close and the owner's close is never\n    33\t   remote -- and attach! never crosses, because it consumes a\n    34\t   descriptor and produces a local handle.\"\n    35\t  #{:dao.stream/descriptor :dao.stream/cursor :dao.stream/next\n    36\t    :dao.stream/append!})\n    37\t\n    38\t\n    39\t(def ^:private protocol-errors\n    40\t  \"The three protocol errors, one answer shape for every operation,\n    41\t   a key instead of an outcome map because no operation's outcome set\n    42\t   can carry them.\"\n    43\t  #{:dao.stream.remote/not-found\n    44\t    :dao.stream.remote/no-surface\n    45\t    :dao.stream.remote/oversize})\n    46\t\n    47\t\n    48\t(defn- well-formed-request?\n    49\t  \"True when `v` is a well-formed request: a map naming its identity\n    50\t   and one of the four wire ops, carrying an args vector and the\n    51\t   asker-minted id. Anything else is dropped as malformed wire input.\"\n    52\t  [v]\n    53\t  (and (map? v)\n    54\t       (contains? v :dao.stream/identity)\n    55\t       (contains? wire-ops (:dao.stream.remote/op v))\n    56\t       (vector? (:dao.stream.remote/args v))\n    57\t       (contains? v :dao.stream.remote/id)))\n    58\t\n    59\t\n    60\t(defn- well-formed-answer?\n    61\t  \"True when `v` is a well-formed answer: a map carrying the id and\n    62\t   the identity and either a protocol error or an outcome map.\"\n    63\t  [v]\n    64\t  (and (map? v)\n    65\t       (contains? v :dao.stream.remote/id)\n    66\t       (contains? v :dao.stream/identity)\n    67\t       (or (contains? protocol-errors (:dao.stream.remote/error v))\n    68\t           (stream/outcome-map? v))))\n    69\t\n    70\t\n    71\t(defn- required-surface\n    72\t  \"The declared surface `op` needs, under the fixed mapping: cursor\n    73\t   and next need :reader, append! needs :writer, descriptor needs no\n    74\t   surface and is always answerable.\"\n    75\t  [op]\n    76\t  (case op\n    77\t    (:dao.stream/cursor :dao.stream/next) :reader\n    78\t    :dao.stream/append! :writer\n    79\t    nil))\n    80\t\n    81\t\n    82\t(defn- error-answer\n    83\t  \"The protocol-error answer shape, with the id and identity the\n    84\t   request carried.\"\n    85\t  [identity id e]\n    86\t  {:dao.stream/identity identity\n    87\t   :dao.stream.remote/id id\n    88\t   :dao.stream.remote/error e})\n    89\t\n    90\t\n    91\t;; =============================================================================\n    92\t;; The mirror step (2.3)\n    93\t;; =============================================================================\n    94\t\n    95\t(defn- valid-budget?\n    96\t  \"True when the request carries the optional next-only budget as a\n    97\t   positive integer. Anything else ignores the budget, which is always\n    98\t   correct for a mirror.\"\n    99\t  [req]\n   100\t  (let [k (:dao.stream.remote/budget req)]\n   101\t    (and (integer? k) (pos? k))))\n   102\t\n   103\t\n   104\t(defn- chase\n   105\t  \"The budget chase: `outcome` is an ok from next; follow the successor\n   106\t   cursor through the same apply-request path at most k - 1 further\n   107\t   times, collecting the further outcome maps in order. The vector ends\n   108\t   at and includes the first non-ok outcome, so blocked, end and gap\n   109\t   reach the reader with the source's own recovery cursor.\"\n   110\t  [h ctx k outcome]\n   111\t  (loop [more [] cur (:dao.stream/cursor outcome) n 1]\n   112\t    (if (or (>= n k) (nil? cur))\n   113\t      more\n   114\t      (let [r (middleware/apply-request h ctx\n   115\t                                        {:dao.stream.remote/op\n   116\t                                         :dao.stream/next\n   117\t                                         :dao.stream.remote/args [cur]})]\n   118\t        (if (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome r))\n   119\t          (recur (conj more r) (:dao.stream/cursor r) (inc n))\n   120\t          (conj more r))))))\n   121\t\n   122\t\n   123\t(defn- write-answer!\n   124\t  \"Append one answer value toward the asker. A value the channel cannot\n   125\t   carry (invalid-value) is answered with the oversize protocol error\n   126\t   in place of that element, the read not skipped and no cursor\n   127\t   advanced past it; any other refused write leaves the request\n   128\t   unanswered.\"\n   129\t  [chan-writer answer]\n   130\t  (let [r (stream/append! chan-writer answer)]\n   131\t    (when (= :dao.stream/invalid-value (:dao.stream/outcome r))\n   132\t      (stream/append! chan-writer\n   133\t                      (error-answer (:dao.stream/identity answer)\n   134\t                                    (:dao.stream.remote/id answer)\n   135\t                                    :dao.stream.remote/oversize)))))\n   136\t\n   137\t\n   138\t(defn- answer!\n   139\t  \"The mirror's steps 1 to 4 for one well-formed request: look the\n   140\t   identity up in the table (not-found); check the op against the\n   141\t   entry's declared surface under the fixed mapping (no-surface);\n   142\t   answer descriptor from the entry's handle directly, adding the\n   143\t   declared surface, and apply cursor, next and append! through\n   144\t   apply-request with the channel context; append the answer with the\n   145\t   id and identity. For next with a budget k, and an ok first outcome,\n   146\t   the chase places the further outcomes under more.\"\n   147\t  [table chan-writer channel req]\n   148\t  (let [identity (:dao.stream/identity req)\n   149\t        id (:dao.stream.remote/id req)\n   150\t        op (:dao.stream.remote/op req)\n   151\t        entry (get table identity)]\n   152\t    (if-not entry\n   153\t      (write-answer! chan-writer\n   154\t                     (error-answer identity id\n   155\t                                   :dao.stream.remote/not-found))\n   156\t      (let [h (:handle entry)]\n   157\t        (if (= :dao.stream/descriptor op)\n   158\t          (write-answer!\n   159\t            chan-writer\n   160\t            (-> (stream/descriptor h)\n   161\t                (assoc :dao.stream.remote/id id\n   162\t                       :dao.stream/identity identity\n   163\t                       :dao.stream.remote/surface (:surface entry))))\n   164\t          (if-not (contains? (into #{} (:surface entry))\n   165\t                             (required-surface op))\n   166\t            (write-answer! chan-writer\n   167\t                           (error-answer identity id\n   168\t                                         :dao.stream.remote/no-surface))\n   169\t            (let [ctx {:dao.stream.remote/channel channel}\n   170\t                  outcome (middleware/apply-request h ctx req)\n   171\t                  more (when (and (= :dao.stream/next op)\n   172\t                                  (valid-budget? req)\n   173\t                                  (= :dao.stream/ok\n   174\t                                     (:dao.stream/outcome outcome)))\n   175\t                         (chase h ctx (:dao.stream.remote/budget req)\n   176\t                                outcome))\n   177\t                  answer (cond-> (assoc outcome\n   178\t                                        :dao.stream.remote/id id\n   179\t                                        :dao.stream/identity identity)\n   180\t                           (seq more) (assoc :dao.stream.remote/more\n   181\t                                             more))]\n   182\t              (write-answer! chan-writer answer))))))))\n   183\t\n   184\t\n   185\t(defn mirror-step\n   186\t  \"The pure step a peer runs over a channel to answer requests against\n   187\t   its table: `(mirror-step table chan-reader cursor chan-writer) ->\n   188\t   cursor'`. For each request read from chan-reader at `cursor`, in\n   189\t   order, the four steps of 2.3 run; the mirror holds no state between\n   190\t   calls beyond the returned cursor. A value that is neither a\n   191\t   well-formed request nor a well-formed answer is dropped as malformed\n   192\t   wire input. The step stops when the channel reader answers blocked\n   193\t   or end; a gap adopts the reader's own recovery cursor, the lost wire\n   194\t   values being lost. The mirror never constructs, parses or rewrites\n   195\t   a cursor, an anchor or an outcome: the served stream is the\n   196\t   original, its cursors, its positions, its gap, its end.\"\n   197\t  [table chan-reader cursor chan-writer]\n   198\t  (let [channel (:dao.stream/identity (stream/descriptor chan-reader))]\n   199\t    (loop [cursor cursor]\n   200\t      (let [r (stream/next chan-reader cursor)]\n   201\t        (case (:dao.stream/outcome r)\n   202\t          :dao.stream/ok\n   203\t          (do (when (well-formed-request? (:dao.stream/value r))\n   204\t                (answer! table chan-writer channel\n   205\t                         (:dao.stream/value r)))\n   206\t              (recur (:dao.stream/cursor r)))\n   207\t          :dao.stream/blocked cursor\n   208\t          :dao.stream/end cursor\n   209\t          :dao.stream/gap (recur (:dao.stream/cursor r))\n   210\t          cursor)))))\n   211\t\n   212\t\n   213\t;; =============================================================================\n   214\t;; The link (2.4, 2.5)\n   215\t;; =============================================================================\n   216\t\n   217\t(defn- new-link\n   218\t  \"One link per channel descriptor, shared by every reflection through\n   219\t   that channel. A link holds the channel writer; the channel reader\n   220\t   and the link's own cursor on it; the outstanding request ids with\n   221\t   the request each was sent for; the attach descriptor probes the\n   222\t   writer has refused, kept unsent with the reflection each was sent\n   223\t   for; filed answers keyed by id; the more outcomes installed at\n   224\t   their served identity and the cursor that precedes each; an\n   225\t   optional event writer; and the policy data -- the resend-after k\n   226\t   and the budget k stamped on next requests, both composition data.\"\n   227\t  [chan policy]\n   228\t  (atom {:reader (:reader chan)\n   229\t         :writer (:writer chan)\n   230\t         :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor\n   231\t                   (stream/cursor (:reader chan) :dao.stream/oldest))\n   232\t         :outstanding {}\n   233\t         :pending {}\n   234\t         :filed {}\n   235\t         :filed-cursors {}\n   236\t         :events (:dao.stream.remote/events policy)\n   237\t         :resend-after (:dao.stream.remote/resend-after policy)\n   238\t         :budget (:dao.stream.remote/budget policy)\n   239\t         :next-id 0\n   240\t         :channel-gone? false}))\n   241\t\n   242\t\n   243\t(defn- mint-id!\n   244\t  \"The next asker-minted id, unique per channel.\"\n   245\t  [link]\n   246\t  (let [n (:next-id @link)]\n   247\t    (swap! link assoc :next-id (inc n))\n   248\t    n))\n   249\t\n   250\t\n   251\t(defn- emit!\n   252\t  \"Append one event to the link's event writer when one is composed;\n   253\t   without an event writer the event is dropped.\"\n   254\t  [link v]\n   255\t  (when-some [w (:events @link)]\n   256\t    (stream/append! w v)))\n   257\t\n   258\t\n   259\t(defn- register!\n   260\t  \"Record `req` as outstanding under its id, with the request it was\n   261\t   sent for, its ask count, and the reflection that sent it.\"\n   262\t  [link refl req]\n   263\t  (let [id (:dao.stream.remote/id req)]\n   264\t    (swap! link assoc-in [:outstanding id]\n   265\t           {:req req :asks 0 :reflection refl})\n   266\t    (swap! refl update :ids conj id)))\n   267\t\n   268\t\n   269\t(defn- wire-request\n   270\t  \"One request map for the channel, its id asker-minted; a next\n   271\t   request carries the link's budget when one is composed.\"\n   272\t  [link refl op args]\n   273\t  (cond-> {:dao.stream/identity (:identity @refl)\n   274\t           :dao.stream.remote/op op\n   275\t           :dao.stream.remote/args args\n   276\t           :dao.stream.remote/id (mint-id! link)}\n   277\t    (and (= :dao.stream/next op) (some? (:budget @link)))\n   278\t    (assoc :dao.stream.remote/budget (:budget @link))))\n   279\t\n   280\t\n   281\t(defn- send-request!\n   282\t  \"Append one request map to the channel writer and register it as\n   283\t   outstanding only when the writer accepted the send: a send the\n   284\t   writer refuses with full leaves the request unsent and nothing\n   285\t   outstanding, so the operation answers as though unanswered and the\n   286\t   next ask sends again. An attach descriptor probe the writer\n   287\t   refuses with full is kept on the link instead, unsent, one retry\n   288\t   per drain until a send is accepted. Returns the writer's outcome.\"\n   289\t  [link refl req]\n   290\t  (let [r (stream/append! (:writer @link) req)\n   291\t        id (:dao.stream.remote/id req)]\n   292\t    (cond\n   293\t      (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome r))\n   294\t      (do (register! link refl req)\n   295\t          (swap! link update :pending dissoc id))\n   296\t\n   297\t      (and (= :dao.stream/full (:dao.stream/outcome r))\n   298\t           (= :dao.stream/descriptor (:dao.stream.remote/op req)))\n   299\t      (swap! link assoc-in [:pending id]\n   300\t             {:req req :reflection refl}))\n   301\t    r))\n   302\t\n   303\t\n   304\t(defn- count-ask!\n   305\t  \"Count one further ask of the outstanding request `id`; on reaching\n   306\t   the link's resend-after k, re-send it once -- the same id, a\n   307\t   duplicate of an idempotent operation, which recomputes the same or a\n   308\t   later, equally true answer -- and start counting afresh. append! is\n   309\t   never re-sent: it never stays outstanding beyond its send.\"\n   310\t  [link id]\n   311\t  (when-some [entry (get (:outstanding @link) id)]\n   312\t    (let [k (:resend-after @link)\n   313\t          asks (inc (:asks entry))]\n   314\t      (swap! link assoc-in [:outstanding id :asks] asks)\n   315\t      (when (and (integer? k) (pos? k) (<= k asks))\n   316\t        (stream/append! (:writer @link) (:req entry))\n   317\t        (swap! link assoc-in [:outstanding id :asks] 0)))))\n   318\t\n   319\t\n   320\t(defn- outstanding-for\n   321\t  \"The id of the link's outstanding request for `op` with `arg` sent by\n   322\t   `refl`, if any; the request each outstanding id was sent for makes\n   323\t   the correlation.\"\n   324\t  [link refl op arg]\n   325\t  (when-some [[id _]\n   326\t              (first\n   327\t                (filter (fn [[_ e]]\n   328\t                          (let [r (:req e)]\n   329\t                            (and (= refl (:reflection e))\n   330\t                                 (= op (:dao.stream.remote/op r))\n   331\t                                 (= arg (first\n   332\t                                          (:dao.stream.remote/args r))))))\n   333\t                        (:outstanding @link)))]\n   334\t    id))\n   335\t\n   336\t\n   337\t(defn- filed-for\n   338\t  \"The first filed answer for `op` with `arg` sent by `refl`, in id\n   339\t   order, as [id entry]. A filed answer keeps the request it was sent\n   340\t   for and the reflection that sent it -- an answer belongs to the\n     1\t(ns dao.stream.udp\n     2\t  \"The UDP channel of dao.stream.remote.md (3.2), rebuilt on the raw\n     3\t   datagram layer (docs/design/dao.stream.datagram.md 7): the WebSocket\n     4\t   deposit model over a socket that retains nothing, with the raw layer\n     5\t   beneath it feeding and carrying it.\n     6\t\n     7\t   `make-port` composes the local socket's protocol state, host-free.\n     8\t   What feeds it is the raw layer: `:send!` is composed by the\n     9\t   composition as a closure that encodes the bytes as Base64 and appends\n    10\t   one outbound value to the raw writer handle\n    11\t   (dao.stream.datagram/writer over a bound host seam), and `send-value!`\n    12\t   answers that raw writer's first non-ok outcome instead of discarding\n    13\t   it. `port-step!` is the reading side: one step reading the raw traffic\n    14\t   ring from the port's own raw cursor to blocked, skipping lifecycle\n    15\t   events and :dao.jing.dht/v maps (the DHT interpreter sharing the\n    16\t   socket, dao.stream.datagram.md 6), decoding each datagram event's\n    17\t   Base64 and calling `receive!` with the source address and bytes. The\n    18\t   composition drives it, one owner per port, before the projections'\n    19\t   `step!`.\n    20\t\n    21\t   One CBOR value, canonical profile, rides one datagram, at most\n    22\t   `max-datagram` bytes, fragment envelope included -- the figure\n    23\t   the DHT also uses to clear common path MTUs. An\n    24\t   encoded message over that budget is split into fragment envelopes,\n    25\t   channel-internal: neither the mirror step nor the link\n    26\t   (dao.stream.remote) ever sees one, only the complete decoded message\n    27\t   `receive!` reassembles and deposits.\n    28\t\n    29\t   Reassembly key, per 3.2: [attachment identity, source address,\n    30\t   direction, id]. For UDP the attachment identity a socket serves IS\n    31\t   the remote host:port it saw, so this port's own reassembly key\n    32\t   collapses the first two components into one address string; a\n    33\t   fragment's own carried id is unique only per asking side, so the\n    34\t   direction distinguishes a fragment split from an outgoing request\n    35\t   from one split from its answer, both landing at the same address\n    36\t   with the same id, when this side is both asker and mirror on one\n    37\t   channel. Bounded by two composition data, no clock:\n    38\t   `:dao.stream.udp/max-message-bytes` (default 64 KiB) refuses an\n    39\t   oversize send and bounds a reassembly's accumulated chunk bytes;\n    40\t   and `:dao.stream.udp/max-partial-messages`, evicting the oldest\n    41\t   partial message.\n    42\t\n    43\t   The deposit adapter shape: every reassembled datagram value is\n    44\t   deposited as one event, `{:dao.stream.udp/attachment id\n    45\t   :dao.stream.udp/source {:dao.stream.udp/host h\n    46\t   :dao.stream.udp/port p} :dao.stream.udp/value v}`, onto the shared\n    47\t   traffic medium the composition wires -- one socket may serve several\n    48\t   attachments, one per remote address, so this medium is shared the\n    49\t   way a WebSocket connection's own traffic medium is not. `projection`\n    50\t   and `step!` are this channel's `ws-project`: driven per attachment\n    51\t   at the composition's cadence, they keep the events naming their own\n    52\t   attachment and append each value onto the channel ring that\n    53\t   attachment's reflections read.\n    54\t\n    55\t   `make-attacher` binds or reuses `:port` and answers a writer handle\n    56\t   whose `append!` sends toward the descriptor's address, fragmenting\n    57\t   and refusing oversize per the above; `send-to!` is the same write\n    58\t   path to an explicit destination from an already-bound port, for hole\n    59\t   punching (section 4), outside any attach!'d descriptor. Replies\n    60\t   always go to a datagram's own source address, never a payload's\n    61\t   claim, because `receive!` is the only place a source address is\n    62\t   read, and every deposited event carries the address the socket\n    63\t   itself saw it arrive from.\"\n    64\t  (:require [dao.stream :as stream]\n    65\t            [dao.stream.base64 :as base64]\n    66\t            [dao.stream.chunks :as chunks]\n    67\t            [dao.stream.cbor :as cbor]\n    68\t            [dao.stream.datagram :as datagram])\n    69\t  #?(:cljd (:import [\"dart:typed_data\" Uint8List])))\n    70\t\n    71\t\n    72\t(def transport-type :dao.stream/udp)\n    73\t\n    74\t\n    75\t(def max-datagram\n    76\t  \"The whole encoded datagram's budget, fragment envelope included: the\n    77\t   1200-byte figure dao.jing.dht also uses to clear common path\n    78\t   MTUs (docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md 3.2).\"\n    79\t  1200)\n    80\t\n    81\t\n    82\t(def default-max-message-bytes\n    83\t  \"64 KiB, the default reassembly maximum (3.2).\"\n    84\t  (* 64 1024))\n    85\t\n    86\t\n    87\t(def default-max-partial-messages\n    88\t  \"No default is named in 3.2; this is this implementation's own bound\n    89\t   on outstanding partial messages, chosen only to keep reassembly\n    90\t   memory finite without a clock.\"\n    91\t  64)\n    92\t\n    93\t\n    94\t(defn descriptor?\n    95\t  \"{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/udp :dao.stream/identity id\n    96\t     :dao.stream.udp/host h :dao.stream.udp/port p} (3.2).\"\n    97\t  [x]\n    98\t  (and (map? x)\n    99\t       (= transport-type (:dao.stream/type x))\n   100\t       (string? (:dao.stream/identity x))\n   101\t       (string? (:dao.stream.udp/host x))\n   102\t       (integer? (:dao.stream.udp/port x))\n   103\t       (pos? (:dao.stream.udp/port x))))\n   104\t\n   105\t\n   106\t(defn address-key\n   107\t  \"The attachment identity a socket serves for one remote address: the\n   108\t   host:port it saw, as a string, so it can key a map.\"\n   109\t  [host port]\n   110\t  (str host \":\" port))\n   111\t\n   112\t\n   113\t;; =============================================================================\n   114\t;; Portable host byte arrays\n   115\t;; =============================================================================\n   116\t\n   117\t(defn- blen\n   118\t  [bs]\n   119\t  #?(:cljd (.-length ^Uint8List bs)\n   120\t     :default (alength bs)))\n   121\t\n   122\t\n   123\t;; =============================================================================\n   124\t;; Fragmentation (write side)\n   125\t;; =============================================================================\n   126\t\n   127\t(defn- direction-of\n   128\t  \"A request carries :dao.stream.remote/op; an answer never does.\"\n   129\t  [v]\n   130\t  (if (contains? v :dao.stream.remote/op)\n   131\t    :dao.stream.udp/request\n   132\t    :dao.stream.udp/answer))\n   133\t\n   134\t\n   135\t(defn- fragment-envelope\n   136\t  [id part parts direction chunk]\n   137\t  {:dao.stream.remote/id id\n   138\t   :dao.stream.udp/part part\n   139\t   :dao.stream.udp/parts parts\n   140\t   :dao.stream.udp/direction direction\n   141\t   :dao.stream.udp/bytes chunk})\n   142\t\n   143\t\n   144\t(defn- fragments\n   145\t  [payload id direction]\n   146\t  (chunks/split payload max-datagram\n   147\t                (fn [part parts bytes]\n   148\t                  (fragment-envelope id part parts direction bytes))\n   149\t                cbor/encode))\n   150\t\n   151\t\n   152\t;; =============================================================================\n   153\t;; The port: shared local-socket protocol state\n   154\t;; =============================================================================\n   155\t\n   156\t(defn make-port\n   157\t  \"Compose one local socket's protocol state. `:send!` is the host seam,\n   158\t   `(send! host port bytes) -> an outcome map or nothing`, called once per\n   159\t   outbound datagram; it must not wait, and a transport failure it answers\n   160\t   travels up as `send-value!`'s outcome -- the raw writer's first non-ok\n   161\t   outcome, not discarded. The composition composes it, over the raw\n   162\t   datagram layer, as the Base64 closure appending one outbound value to\n   163\t   `dao.stream.datagram/writer` (dao.stream.datagram.md 7). `:traffic` is\n   164\t   the shared deposit target the composition wires -- a writer every\n   165\t   reassembled datagram's event is appended to (`dao.stream/writer?`).\n   166\t   `:raw-traffic` is the raw datagram layer's traffic ring the port reads\n   167\t   itself a cursor on, minted :oldest at composition time so a raw gap\n   168\t   reports lost datagrams -- `port-step!` requires it. `:max-message-bytes`\n   169\t   and `:max-partial-messages` are the two reassembly bounds (defaults\n   170\t   above).\"\n   171\t  [{:keys [send! traffic raw-traffic max-message-bytes\n   172\t           max-partial-messages]}]\n   173\t  (when-not (and (fn? send!) (stream/writer? traffic))\n   174\t    (throw (ex-info \"invalid dao.stream.udp port composition\"\n   175\t                    {:send! send! :traffic traffic})))\n   176\t  (when (and (some? raw-traffic) (not (stream/reader? raw-traffic)))\n   177\t    (throw (ex-info \"invalid dao.stream.udp raw traffic composition\"\n   178\t                    {:raw-traffic raw-traffic})))\n   179\t  (atom {:send! send!\n   180\t         :traffic traffic\n   181\t         :raw-traffic raw-traffic\n   182\t         :raw-cursor (when raw-traffic\n   183\t                       (:dao.stream/cursor\n   184\t                         (stream/cursor raw-traffic stream/anchor-oldest)))\n   185\t         :max-message-bytes (or max-message-bytes\n   186\t                                default-max-message-bytes)\n   187\t         :max-partial-messages (or max-partial-messages\n   188\t                                   default-max-partial-messages)\n   189\t         :partial {}\n   190\t         :partial-order []}))\n   191\t\n   192\t\n   193\t(defn- send-datagram!\n   194\t  \"One datagram out through the composed :send! seam: the seam's own\n   195\t   outcome map travels up -- the raw writer's first non-ok outcome, not\n   196\t   discarded -- and a fire-and-forget seam answering nothing is ok.\"\n   197\t  [port host dest-port bytes]\n   198\t  (let [r ((:send! @port) host dest-port bytes)]\n   199\t    (if (and (map? r) (contains? r :dao.stream/outcome))\n   200\t      r\n   201\t      {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok})))\n   202\t\n   203\t\n   204\t(defn send-value!\n   205\t  \"Encode and send `v` toward `host`:`dest-port` from `port`'s socket:\n   206\t   a single datagram when it fits the budget, fragments when it does\n   207\t   not, transport-error naming oversize when the encoded message\n   208\t   exceeds :dao.stream.udp/max-message-bytes -- never sent, torn or\n   209\t   partially sent. The seam's first non-ok outcome is the answer: a\n   210\t   fragment run stops there, cleanly, nothing torn following it.\"\n   211\t  [port host dest-port v]\n   212\t  (let [payload (cbor/encode v)\n   213\t        n (blen payload)]\n   214\t    (cond\n   215\t      (> n (:max-message-bytes @port))\n   216\t      {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\n   217\t       :dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/oversize}\n   218\t\n   219\t      (<= n max-datagram)\n   220\t      (send-datagram! port host dest-port payload)\n   221\t\n   222\t      :else\n   223\t      (let [id (:dao.stream.remote/id v)\n   224\t            direction (direction-of v)]\n   225\t        (loop [frags (fragments payload id direction)]\n   226\t          (if (empty? frags)\n   227\t            {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}\n   228\t            (let [r (send-datagram! port host dest-port\n   229\t                                    (cbor/encode (first frags)))]\n   230\t              (if (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome r))\n   231\t                (recur (rest frags))\n   232\t                r))))))))\n   233\t\n   234\t\n   235\t(defn send-to!\n   236\t  \"The explicit-destination write path from an already-bound port,\n   237\t   outside any attach!'d descriptor -- hole punching depends on it\n   238\t   (3.2, section 4).\"\n   239\t  [port host dest-port v]\n   240\t  (send-value! port host dest-port v))\n   241\t\n   242\t\n   243\t;; =============================================================================\n   244\t;; Reassembly (read side)\n   245\t;; =============================================================================\n   246\t\n   247\t(defn- fragment-envelope?\n   248\t  \"Any reserved fragment key present makes a datagram a fragment\n   249\t   attempt: it must then validate fully or be dropped -- a shape\n   250\t   carrying :parts/:direction/:bytes but missing :part does not fall\n   251\t   through to the deposit path.\"\n   252\t  [v]\n   253\t  (and (map? v)\n   254\t       (some #(contains? v %)\n   255\t             [:dao.stream.udp/part :dao.stream.udp/parts\n   256\t              :dao.stream.udp/direction :dao.stream.udp/bytes])))\n   257\t\n   258\t\n   259\t(defn- well-formed-fragment?\n   260\t  \"A fragment envelope's own wire fields, checked before any reassembly\n   261\t   state can change: :part and :parts are integers with 0 <= :part <\n   262\t   :parts, 1 <= :parts, :direction one of the two the write side\n   263\t   mints, and :bytes a host byte payload. A datagram failing this is\n   264\t   best-effort wire input, dropped below the mirror and the link like\n   265\t   any other malformed one -- nothing here may throw during\n   266\t   reassembly, whatever a peer put on the wire.\"\n   267\t  [v]\n   268\t  (let [{:dao.stream.udp/keys [part parts direction bytes]} v]\n   269\t    (and (integer? part)\n   270\t         (integer? parts)\n   271\t         (pos? parts)\n   272\t         (<= 0 part)\n   273\t         (< part parts)\n   274\t         (#{:dao.stream.udp/request :dao.stream.udp/answer} direction)\n   275\t         (cbor/byte-payload? bytes))))\n   276\t\n   277\t\n   278\t(defn- absorb-fragment!\n   279\t  [port attachment frag]\n   280\t  (let [{:dao.stream.remote/keys [id]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
