for every counted peer, the cookie that peer's\n976:  fresh reply carried; a store with a missing or wrong cookie causes no work.\n977:- The need-cookie reply never encodes to more than `first-contact-bytes`, and\n978:  a cookie-less datagram shorter than that reply is answered with silence.\n1000:  chunks stay within every bound.\n1012:**S4: hardening, lookup repair, storage bound.** Depends on S3. The keyed\n1013:`cookie-for` replacing the S2 stand-in, the chunk path under the gate, and\n1014:every bound. Acceptance:\n1015:- A cookie cannot be computed without the secret; the S2 stand-in is gone.\n1018:  Tested across datagram sizes from 1 byte to the budget, chunks included.\n1023:- Inbound storage at its bound refuses explicitly.\n1061:  (default 0), `:max-inbound-bytes` (default 64 MiB), `:bind!` (the host\n1063:  root secret at join: 32 CSPRNG bytes, held only in the node value.\n1067:  - `:bound`, `:bind-failed`;\n1081:  paces the replicate requests below `max-pending-writes` through a bounded\n1101:reconciliation sweeps, NAT meeting, a browser transport, latest-root\n1107:failure reasons as data), adds a bounded replicate backlog so a round's\n292:A name is resolved from signed envelopes in explicitly loaded index snapshots,\n726:reason for that address (<code>/exhausted</code>, <code>/deadline</code>,\n786:  and publishes only when none remain: no HEAD write and no announcement\n792:- Restored history is not re-materialized on rehydration. An index\n952:  each once, sequenced in that order. Whole programs are taken; a\n983:published at the REPL is announced by the round's HEAD write, with the\n1283:  or by the DHT's `ack-ticks` deadline. So each offered blob of each\n1408:- **The announced index is unaffected.** HEAD moves when the round's blobs\n1439: :yin.module/seq         41}\n1445: :yin.module/seq         42}\n1504:                  :seq-floor 0}}\n1586:- Tamper cases, each `:bad-proof`: a changed name, manifest, sequence,\n1626:**The sequence is deriv