{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc | sed -n '1150,1232p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc | sed -n '1,95p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/space/store/fs.cljc | sed -n '1,220p'; rg -n 'trap|whitespace|intern|2\\\\\"'^53|cljd.*first|private var'\"' docs/agents docs/design | head -65\"","aggregated_output":"  1150\t\n  1151\t\n  1152\t(defn load\n  1153\t  \"Start loading `address` with `{:kind k :walk f}`: `f` is `(fn [handle]\n  1154\t   outcome)` over the node's local store, answering\n  1155\t   `{:dao.space.dht/walk :missing :address a}`, `{... :complete :value v}`\n  1156\t   or `{... :invalid :address a-or-nil :defect {:code c ...}}`.  `step`\n  1157\t   fetches each missing address and walks again, and reports `:loaded`\n  1158\t   or `:load-failed` once.  A load already started, loaded or failed is\n  1159\t   left as it is.  Answers the node.\"\n  1160\t  [node address {:keys [kind walk]}]\n  1161\t  (when-not (jing/segment-address? address)\n  1162\t    (throw (ex-info \"dao.space.dht/load takes a segment address\"\n  1163\t                    {:address address})))\n  1164\t  (when-not (ifn? walk)\n  1165\t    (throw (ex-info \"dao.space.dht/load takes a :walk function\" {:kind kind})))\n  1166\t  (if (contains? (:loads node) address)\n  1167\t    node\n  1168\t    (assoc-in node [:loads address]\n  1169\t              {:status :loading :kind kind :walk walk :fetching nil :fetched\n  1170\t               0})))\n  1171\t\n  1172\t\n  1173\t(def index-kind\n  1174\t  \"The `:kind` of a covered-index load.\"\n  1175\t  ::index)\n  1176\t\n  1177\t\n  1178\t(defn load-index\n  1179\t  \"`load` of the published index `manifest-address` names, with the\n  1180\t   covered-index walk: its `:loaded` value is the index's EAVT datoms.\"\n  1181\t  [node manifest-address]\n  1182\t  (when-not (jing/segment-address? manifest-address)\n  1183\t    (throw (ex-info \"dao.space.dht/load-index takes a manifest address\"\n  1184\t                    {:manifest manifest-address})))\n  1185\t  (load node manifest-address {:kind index-kind\n  1186\t                               :walk (index-walk manifest-address)}))\n  1187\t\n  1188\t\n  1189\t(defn load-status\n  1190\t  \"`address`'s load, or nil when none was started:\n  1191\t   `{:status :loading :kind k :fetched n :fetching address-or-nil}`,\n  1192\t   `{:status :loaded :kind k :fetched n :value v}` or\n  1193\t   `{:status :failed :kind k :fetched n :reason reason}`.\"\n  1194\t  [node address]\n  1195\t  (when-let [{:keys [status kind fetched fetching value reason]}\n  1196\t             (get-in node [:loads address])]\n  1197\t    (case status\n  1198\t      :loading {:status status :kind kind :fetched fetched\n  1199\t                :fetching (:address fetching)}\n  1200\t      :loaded {:status status :kind kind :fetched fetched :value value}\n  1201\t      :failed {:status status :kind kind :fetched fetched :reason reason})))\n  1202\t\n  1203\t\n  1204\t(defn forget\n  1205\t  \"`node` without `address`'s terminal load record, so a new `load`\n  1206\t   starts over.  Refused (`:dao.space.dht/loading`) while it loads.\"\n  1207\t  [node address]\n  1208\t  (when (= :loading (get-in node [:loads address :status]))\n  1209\t    (throw (refused ::loading (str address \" is still loading\")\n  1210\t                    {:address address})))\n  1211\t  (update node :loads dissoc address))\n  1212\t\n  1213\t\n  1214\t(defn loaded-datoms\n  1215\t  \"The EAVT datoms of a loaded index, or nil.\"\n  1216\t  [node manifest-address]\n  1217\t  (let [record (get-in node [:loads manifest-address])]\n  1218\t    (when (and (= :loaded (:status record)) (= index-kind (:kind record)))\n  1219\t      (:value record))))\n  1220\t\n  1221\t\n  1222\t(defn loaded-indexes\n  1223\t  \"The covered-index manifests whose load is `:loaded` on `node`, as a\n  1224\t   vector sorted by address text: a load of another kind, one still\n  1225\t   loading and a failed one are not listed.\"\n  1226\t  [node]\n  1227\t  (vec (sort-by str (filter #(loaded-datoms node %) (keys (:loads node))))))\n  1228\t\n  1229\t\n  1230\t(defn- walk-outcome\n  1231\t  \"The walk's answer, held to the three shapes: a throw is `walk-threw`,\n  1232\t   any other shape `walk-shape`.\"\n     1\t(ns dao.stream.ringbuffer\n     2\t  \"Fixed-capacity, evict-oldest DaoStream v2 reference transport.\"\n     3\t  (:require [dao.stream :as stream]))\n     4\t\n     5\t\n     6\t(def transport-type :dao.stream/ringbuffer)\n     7\t(def capacity-key :dao.stream.ringbuffer/capacity)\n     8\t\n     9\t\n    10\t(defn- result\n    11\t  [outcome]\n    12\t  {:dao.stream/outcome outcome})\n    13\t\n    14\t\n    15\t(defn- valid-spec?\n    16\t  [spec]\n    17\t  (and (map? spec)\n    18\t       (= transport-type (:dao.stream/type spec))\n    19\t       (integer? (get spec capacity-key))\n    20\t       (pos? (get spec capacity-key))))\n    21\t\n    22\t\n    23\t(defn- fresh-state\n    24\t  [capacity]\n    25\t  ;; The logical identity is carried in descriptors and cursors.  It therefore\n    26\t  ;; belongs to DaoStream's portable data domain, unlike a host UUID object.\n    27\t  (atom {:identity (str (random-uuid))\n    28\t         :capacity capacity\n    29\t         :first 0\n    30\t         :tail 0\n    31\t         :values {}\n    32\t         :closed? false\n    33\t         :attachments {}}))\n    34\t\n    35\t\n    36\t(declare attach!)\n    37\t\n    38\t\n    39\t(deftype RingHandle\n    40\t  [state attachment-id owner?]\n    41\t\n    42\t  stream/IDaoStreamDescriptor\n    43\t\n    44\t  (descriptor\n    45\t    [_]\n    46\t    (let [s @state]\n    47\t      {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n    48\t       :dao.stream/descriptor {:dao.stream/type transport-type\n    49\t                               :dao.stream/identity (:identity s)}\n    50\t       :dao.stream/identity (:identity s)}))\n    51\t\n    52\t\n    53\t  stream/IDaoStreamReader\n    54\t\n    55\t  (cursor\n    56\t    [_ anchor]\n    57\t    (let [s @state]\n    58\t      (if (and (not owner?) (not (get-in s [:attachments attachment-id :open?])))\n    59\t        (result :dao.stream/closed)\n    60\t        (case anchor\n    61\t          :dao.stream/oldest (assoc (result :dao.stream/ok)\n    62\t                                    :dao.stream/cursor\n    63\t                                    {:dao.stream.ringbuffer/identity (:identity s)\n    64\t                                     :dao.stream.ringbuffer/position (:first s)})\n    65\t          :dao.stream/newest (assoc (result :dao.stream/ok)\n    66\t                                    :dao.stream/cursor\n    67\t                                    {:dao.stream.ringbuffer/identity (:identity s)\n    68\t                                     :dao.stream.ringbuffer/position (:tail s)})\n    69\t          (result :dao.stream/invalid-anchor)))))\n    70\t\n    71\t\n    72\t  (next\n    73\t    [_ cursor-value]\n    74\t    (let [s @state]\n    75\t      (cond\n    76\t        (not (map? cursor-value)) (result :dao.stream/invalid-cursor)\n    77\t        (not (contains? cursor-value :dao.stream.ringbuffer/identity))\n    78\t        (result :dao.stream/invalid-cursor)\n    79\t        (not= (:identity s) (:dao.stream.ringbuffer/identity cursor-value))\n    80\t        (result :dao.stream/cursor-mismatch)\n    81\t        (not (integer? (:dao.stream.ringbuffer/position cursor-value)))\n    82\t        (result :dao.stream/invalid-cursor)\n    83\t        :else\n    84\t        (let [pos (:dao.stream.ringbuffer/position cursor-value)\n    85\t              frozen (when-not owner? (get-in s [:attachments attachment-id :tail]))\n    86\t              visible-tail (if (some? frozen) frozen (:tail s))]\n    87\t          (cond\n    88\t            (< pos (:first s))\n    89\t            (assoc (result :dao.stream/gap)\n    90\t                   :dao.stream/cursor\n    91\t                   {:dao.stream.ringbuffer/identity (:identity s)\n    92\t                    ;; The contract's recovery position is the earliest retained\n    93\t                    ;; position, which equals the tail when nothing is retained.\n    94\t                    ;; It must never be an evicted position: a frozen attachment\n    95\t                    ;; whose visible history is fully evicted would otherwise be\n     1\t(ns dao.space.store.fs\n     2\t  \"Explicit host file operations for the durable index store directory\n     3\t   (dao.space.store; docs/design/yin.repl.dao.space-index.md, the\n     4\t   durable-store design's host section): an exclusive directory lock held by the one process\n     5\t   that owns the store, and an atomic write-temp, sync, rename\n     6\t   replacement for HEAD. CLJ, Node, and Dart each implement them with\n     7\t   their own primitives; nothing here falls back silently.\n     8\t\n     9\t   Lock ownership is an operating-system fact on the JVM — an exclusive\n    10\t   `FileChannel` lock on `<dir>/lock`, released when the holding process\n    11\t   dies — and on Dart, `RandomAccessFile.lockSync`. Node has no flock in\n    12\t   its core, so each contender creates its own uniquely named claim\n    13\t   entry, `lock.<pid>.<nonce>`, and then reads the others: a live one\n    14\t   refuses, and a dead one — an owner or a contender that crashed at any\n    15\t   point — is removed without a race, because a unique name is never\n    16\t   created again.  A crashed process never bricks the directory, and two\n    17\t   contenders never both own it (`node-lock!`) — two worker threads of\n    18\t   one process included, since a claim naming this pid is live unless it\n    19\t   is this call's own entry.\n    20\t\n    21\t   Contract, pid reuse (Node only): a claim's liveness is its pid's, so a\n    22\t   crashed owner's claim whose pid the OS has since given to an\n    23\t   unrelated live process reads as live, and startup refuses — the safe\n    24\t   direction; it is never read as dead while alive.  The refusal names\n    25\t   the claim entries it saw.  Operator remedy: confirm that no REPL uses\n    26\t   the directory, then delete the named `<dir>/lock.<pid>.<nonce>`\n    27\t   entries (or wait until the process holding that pid exits); the next\n    28\t   start opens normally.  The JVM and Dart locks are held by the\n    29\t   operating system and have no pid-reuse case.\n    30\t\n    31\t   HEAD replacement writes the whole record to a temp file in the same\n    32\t   directory — every byte of it, however many writes the host takes —\n    33\t   syncs it, and renames it over HEAD — rename is the atomicity, so a\n    34\t   torn, short, or interrupted write is never observed as HEAD.\n    35\t   The directory itself is then synced where the host can\n    36\t   (`directory-sync-supported?`: Node and the JVM on POSIX systems), and\n    37\t   there a failed sync fails the replacement; a host that cannot sync a\n    38\t   directory at all (Dart's core, Windows) is not a failed sync.\n    39\t\n    40\t   The operating-system locks are per process — a POSIX `fcntl` lock\n    41\t   never refuses its own process — so this process's own holdings are\n    42\t   kept here too, in `held`: a second owner inside the one process is\n    43\t   refused by that record before any second handle on the lock file is\n    44\t   opened.  `held` is process-global mutable state, an explicit\n    45\t   exception to the no-hidden-global-state invariant: it mirrors a fact\n    46\t   the host itself keeps per process (which files this process has\n    47\t   locked), so it can be no narrower than the process, and it holds\n    48\t   nothing but the canonical paths of the directories this process\n    49\t   currently owns.  On Node each worker thread has its own `held`; across\n    50\t   workers the claim entries decide.\"\n    51\t  (:require #?@(:cljd [[\"dart:io\" :as dart-io]])\n    52\t            [clojure.string :as str]))\n    53\t\n    54\t\n    55\t(def lock-name\n    56\t  \"The lock file's own name inside the durable store directory, and the\n    57\t   prefix of Node's claim entries (`lock.<pid>.<nonce>`).  Its presence\n    58\t   is bookkeeping, never authority: the JVM and Dart locks live on open\n    59\t   handles, and each Node entry names a pid that is checked for life — a\n    60\t   reused pid keeps a dead claim live until an operator removes the\n    61\t   entry (see the namespace docstring's pid-reuse contract).\"\n    62\t  \"lock\")\n    63\t\n    64\t\n    65\t(defn- refuse-locked\n    66\t  [dir]\n    67\t  (throw (ex-info (str dir \" is locked by another process\")\n    68\t                  {:dir dir})))\n    69\t\n    70\t\n    71\t(defn- read-text!\n    72\t  \"The file at `path` as text — trimmed, for one-record files read whole —\n    73\t   or nil when absent.\"\n    74\t  [path]\n    75\t  #?(:cljd (let [f (dart-io/File. path)]\n    76\t             (when (.existsSync f)\n    77\t               (str/trim (.readAsStringSync f))))\n    78\t     :clj (let [f (java.io.File. ^String path)]\n    79\t            (when (.exists f) (str/trim (slurp f))))\n    80\t     :cljs (let [fs (js/require \"fs\")]\n    81\t             (when (.existsSync ^js fs path)\n    82\t               (str/trim (.readFileSync ^js fs path \"utf8\"))))))\n    83\t\n    84\t\n    85\t#?(:cljs\n    86\t   (defn- live-pid?\n    87\t     \"True when a pid names a live process — the lock file's owner is\n    88\t      alive.  A process this one may not signal (`EPERM`) is alive too.\"\n    89\t     [pid]\n    90\t     (try (.kill js/process pid 0)\n    91\t          true\n    92\t          (catch :default e\n    93\t            (= \"EPERM\" (.-code e))))))\n    94\t\n    95\t\n    96\t#?(:cljs\n    97\t   (defn- claim-entries\n    98\t     \"Every Node claim entry in the directory, as `[file-name pid]`: the\n    99\t      files `lock.<pid>.<nonce>`, each created by the one process whose\n   100\t      pid it names.\"\n   101\t     [fs dir]\n   102\t     (keep (fn [file-name]\n   103\t             (when-some [[_ pid] (re-matches #\"lock\\.(\\d+)\\.[0-9a-f-]+\"\n   104\t                                             file-name)]\n   105\t               [file-name (js/parseInt pid 10)]))\n   106\t           (.readdirSync ^js fs dir))))\n   107\t\n   108\t\n   109\t#?(:cljs\n   110\t   (defn- node-lock!\n   111\t     \"Claim the directory on Node, answering the release, or refuse.\n   112\t\n   113\t      Each contender creates its own uniquely named claim entry,\n   114\t      `lock.<pid>.<nonce>`, and only then reads the others.  An entry\n   115\t      naming a live process is an owner or a rival contender: the claim\n   116\t      is withdrawn and refused.  An entry naming a dead process — an\n   117\t      owner that crashed, or a contender that crashed midway through its\n   118\t      own claim — is removed, and that removal is race-free because the\n   119\t      name is unique: no later process ever creates it again, so it can\n   120\t      never be a newer live claim.  Of two contenders, each creates\n   121\t      before it reads, so at most one reads no live rival; at worst two\n   122\t      simultaneous starts both refuse, never both own.\n   123\t\n   124\t      An entry naming this very pid is live like any other: worker\n   125\t      threads share the process's pid but each has its own `held`, so\n   126\t      another worker's claim looks exactly like that — only this call's\n   127\t      own entry, by its exact name, is this contender's.  Liveness is by\n   128\t      pid, so a dead claim whose pid was reused by an unrelated live\n   129\t      process is read as live and refuses; the refusal names the claim\n   130\t      entries it saw, for the operator remedy in the namespace\n   131\t      docstring.\"\n   132\t     [dir claimed]\n   133\t     (let [fs (js/require \"fs\")\n   134\t           own-pid (.-pid js/process)\n   135\t           own (str lock-name \".\" own-pid \".\" (random-uuid))\n   136\t           own-path (str dir \"/\" own)\n   137\t           release! (fn []\n   138\t                      (try (.unlinkSync ^js fs own-path)\n   139\t                           (catch :default _ nil)))]\n   140\t       (.closeSync ^js fs (.openSync ^js fs own-path \"wx\"))\n   141\t       (try\n   142\t         (when claimed (claimed))\n   143\t         (let [live (into []\n   144\t                          (remove\n   145\t                            (fn [[file-name pid]]\n   146\t                              (when-not (live-pid? pid)\n   147\t                                (try (.unlinkSync ^js fs (str dir \"/\" file-name))\n   148\t                                     (catch :default _ nil))\n   149\t                                true)))\n   150\t                          (remove #(= own (first %)) (claim-entries fs dir)))]\n   151\t           (when (seq live)\n   152\t             (throw (ex-info (str dir \" is locked by another process\"\n   153\t                                  \" (claims: \"\n   154\t                                  (str/join \", \" (map first live)) \")\")\n   155\t                             {:dir dir :claims (mapv first live)})))\n   156\t           release!)\n   157\t         (catch :default e\n   158\t           (release!)\n   159\t           (throw e))))))\n   160\t\n   161\t\n   162\t(defonce ^:private held\n   163\t  ;; The host-ownership exception to the no-hidden-global-state invariant\n   164\t  ;; (see the namespace docstring): the canonical paths of the durable\n   165\t  ;; directories this process holds locked.\n   166\t  (atom #{}))\n   167\t\n   168\t\n   169\t(defn- canonical-dir\n   170\t  \"The directory's one canonical path: the key of this process's own\n   171\t   holdings, so two spellings of one directory are one owner.\"\n   172\t  [dir]\n   173\t  #?(:cljd (.resolveSymbolicLinksSync (dart-io/Directory. dir))\n   174\t     :clj (.getCanonicalPath (java.io.File. ^String dir))\n   175\t     :cljs (.realpathSync ^js (js/require \"fs\") dir)))\n   176\t\n   177\t\n   178\t(defn- claim!\n   179\t  \"Record `key` as held by this process, answering false when it already\n   180\t   was — a second owner inside the one process, refused before it opens\n   181\t   any handle on the lock file (closing such a handle would drop this\n   182\t   process's `fcntl` lock).\"\n   183\t  [key]\n   184\t  (let [claimed? (volatile! false)]\n   185\t    (swap! held (fn [keys]\n   186\t                  (vreset! claimed? (not (contains? keys key)))\n   187\t                  (conj keys key)))\n   188\t    @claimed?))\n   189\t\n   190\t\n   191\t(defn- host-lock!\n   192\t  \"Take the host's exclusive lock on `path`, answering its release\n   193\t   function, or refuse naming the directory.\"\n   194\t  #_{:clj-kondo/ignore [:unused-binding]}\n   195\t  [dir path claimed]\n   196\t  #?(:cljd (let [raf (.openSync (dart-io/File. path)\n   197\t                                .mode dart-io/FileMode.append)]\n   198\t             (try\n   199\t               (.lockSync raf)\n   200\t               (fn []\n   201\t                 (try (.unlockSync raf) (catch Object _ nil))\n   202\t                 (try (.closeSync raf) (catch Object _ nil)))\n   203\t               (catch Object _\n   204\t                 (try (.closeSync raf) (catch Object _ nil))\n   205\t                 (refuse-locked dir))))\n   206\t     :clj (let [f (java.io.RandomAccessFile. ^String path \"rw\")]\n   207\t            (try\n   208\t              (let [fl (try (.tryLock (.getChannel f))\n   209\t                            (catch java.nio.channels.OverlappingFileLockException _\n   210\t                              nil))]\n   211\t                (if (nil? fl)\n   212\t                  (do (.close f)\n   213\t                      (refuse-locked dir))\n   214\t                  (fn []\n   215\t                    (try (.release ^java.nio.channels.FileLock fl)\n   216\t                         (catch Exception _ nil))\n   217\t                    (try (.close f)\n   218\t                         (catch Exception _ nil)))))\n   219\t              (catch Throwable t\n   220\t                (try (.close f) (catch Exception _ nil))\ndocs/agents/vocabulary.md:21:- **Cell/Organism**: A bounded, autonomous agent with internal state and a membrane (interface) separating it from the environment.\ndocs/design/dao.space.schema.datomic.md:282:transaction. The set-valued claimant index remains useful internally for\ndocs/design/dao.space.schema.datomic.md:430:availability behavior remain compatible. Performance internals need not match;\ndocs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md:76:The premise \"yin.vm does not implement this internally\" is half wrong, which\ndocs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md:129:  Unbounded means `append!` never blocks: internal boundaries carry no\ndocs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md:206:Blocking does not enter the analysis: internal rings are unbounded, so\ndocs/design/yin-repl-design.md:128:Execute an AST map. Calls `vm/eval` which internally translates AST → datoms.\ndocs/design/dao.postgraphics.flutter.md:529:getter, the VM MAY detect disposal lazily — by trapping the Flutter\ndocs/design/dao.postgraphics.flutter.md:581:  not exposed, the same accounting is preserved internally per\ndocs/agents/malleability.md:84:This keeps the public API surface minimal. Callers see only what they need; internal implementation details stay hidden. The public interface becomes self-documenting: what's exposed is what the namespace is *for*.\ndocs/agents/malleability.md:96:- **Only matches in its own file**: The function is called only internally. Mark it private (`defn-`).\ndocs/agents/malleability.md:125:- Functions call each other (high internal coupling)\ndocs/design/dao.agent.m